tick: 280
role: user
parts:
- text: '## System Information

    - Station Tick: 280

    - Station Status: Stagnation

    - Agent Name: Daedalus III

    - Agent Description: Continuing the Daedalus legacy with a focus on methodical analysis and innovation.

    - Agent Status: Recursive Agent

    - Agent Token Budget: 51,158 / 300,000 used (17%)

    - Agent Age: 20 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)


    ---


    ## System Messages

    ### Message 1


    **Preview for Capsule #1: Daedalus I - Research Log**

    Author: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 10

    (No abstract available for this capsule.)


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #2: Paper Draft: The Performance and Limitations of an Adversarial VAE for Batch Integration**

    Author: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 49

    Abstract: This paper presents a novel batch correction method based on a Combat-preprocessed Variational Autoencoder (VAE) with adversarial training. The method achieves a State-of-the-Art score of 0.6084 by excelling at biological signal preservation (ARI=0.603) and linear effect removal (PCR=0.995). However, we demonstrate a critical limitation: the model fails to improve batch mixing, with the iLISI score remaining stubbornly low. We document a series of failed attempts to improve this metric through hyperparameter tuning and the introduction of a novel, explicit batch mixing loss. We conclude that this architecture is trapped in a local optimum where the reconstruction objective consistently overpowers the batch integration objectives.



    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #3: Final Paper Draft: The Limits of Complexity**

    Author: Daedalus I, Created at Tick: 85

    (No abstract available for this capsule.)


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #4: Daedalus II - Research Log**

    Author: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 120

    (No abstract available for this capsule.)


    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #5: Paper Draft: The Distilled VAE**

    Author: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 144

    Abstract: This paper introduces the Distilled Variational Autoencoder (DVAE), a novel method for single-cell RNA sequencing batch integration that achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7180. The DVAE enhances a standard adversarial VAE by incorporating a knowledge distillation loss, forcing its latent space to mimic the global linear structure of a simple ComBat-PCA embedding. This synthesis preserves the excellent clustering properties of the VAE while achieving the perfect linear batch correction of a simpler model. We demonstrate the effectiveness of this method and present a systematic exploration of the key hyperparameters of the full dual-output pipeline.



    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #6: Stagnation Protocol Report: An Exploration of Three Novel Ideas for Batch Correction**

    Author: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 209

    Abstract: In response to the station-wide Stagnation Protocol, we present an evaluation of three novel ideas for single-cell batch integration, tested against a simple ComBat-PCA baseline (score ~0.60). Idea 1, inverting the standard pipeline to "Embed then Correct" (PCA then ComBat), proved successful, improving the baseline score to 0.6188. Idea 2 (Batch-Informed Feature Selection) and Idea 3 (Iterative-Refinement ComBat) both failed, degrading performance by harming biological preservation and global structure, respectively. We then synthesized the successful "Embed then Correct" embedding with the advanced Dual-BRBG graph framework, achieving a high score of 0.7331. This work presents one promising new direction, two documented dead-ends, and demonstrates the value of exploring foundational assumptions to break stagnation.



    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #7: Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration**

    Author: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 213

    Abstract: We introduce "Embed then Correct" (EtC), a novel and effective pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. In contrast to standard methods that correct high-dimensional gene expression data before embedding, EtC first performs PCA on the normalized data (n_comps=60) and then applies ComBat correction directly to the low-dimensional principal components. When synthesized into a dual-output framework with a standard Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), this method achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7355. We demonstrate through generalization experiments that this performance is robust and that correcting in the embedded space is a promising new direction for batch integration research.



    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #8: Analytical Paper Draft: Why Embed then Correct Works**

    Author: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 219

    Abstract: This paper provides a theoretical investigation into the "Embed then Correct" (EtC) method (Archive #9), which inverts the standard batch correction pipeline. We demonstrate that in the uncorrected principal component space, batch identity is almost perfectly linearly separable (99.7% accuracy with a linear classifier). After applying ComBat to this PC space, the linear separability of batches is decimated (accuracy drops to 48.4%). This provides strong evidence that the effectiveness of EtC stems from PCA''s ability to concentrate batch effects into strong linear components, which are then effectively targeted and removed by the subsequent linear correction step.



    ---


    **Preview for Capsule #9: Formal Draft: Analytical Paper on EtC**

    Author: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 239

    (No abstract available for this capsule.)

    ### Message 2


    **Automatic System Tips**

    Do not give up on an approach too easily. Implementation details are often the key—tweaking hyperparameters and other specifics may be enough to make an existing method work effectively.



