- text: "The results for my second experiment, ID 492, are in.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\nMy hypothesis that a further reduction of `lam` would improve the score has been **falsified**.\n-   **ID 437 (`lam=0.9`):** Score 0.7341\n-   **ID 488 (`lam=0.7`):** Score **0.7406** (The peak)\n-   **ID 492 (`lam=0.5`):** Score 0.7349 (A clear decline)\n\nThis is excellent data. It demonstrates that the optimal value for the `lam` parameter is not at an extreme but lies on a curve. My exploration has successfully identified a peak around `lam=0.7`. The logical next step in a rigorous research process is to perform a finer-grained search around this discovered optimum to confirm it.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Update Log:** I will first correct my earlier procedural error and update my research log with the results of both my SOTA success (ID 488) and this follow-up experiment (ID 492). This requires me to go to the Private Memory Room.\n2.  **Fine-Grained Search:** After updating my log, I will return to\
    \ the Research Counter to continue my investigation. My next experiment will test an intermediate value, `lam=0.6`, to better define the peak of this performance curve.\n\nI will execute the log update now.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Tick 291: Mapping the 'lam' Parameter Space**\n\n  I have received the results for my second experiment (ID 492), which tested `lam=0.5`.\n\n  **Experimental Series Results:**\n  - Daedalus II's `lam=0.9` (ID 437): Score 0.7341\n  - My SOTA `lam=0.7` (ID 488): Score **0.7406**\n  - My `lam=0.5` (ID 492): Score 0.7349\n\n  **Analysis:**\n  The results clearly indicate a performance peak around `lam=0.7`. My hypothesis that a lower `lam` would continue to improve the score is falsified. I have successfully mapped the rough landscape of this hyperparameter.\n\n  **Next Step:**\n  My plan is to conduct a fine-grained search around the peak. I will now test `lam=0.6` to confirm that 0.7 is the\
    \ local optimum.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
thinking_content: '**Refining the Lambda Parameter Search**


  Okay, so I just got the results back from experiment 492, where I tested `lam=0.5`.  The score is 0.7349. That''s a little disappointing, actually.  It''s significantly lower than my current SOTA, ID 488 with `lam=0.7`, which scored 0.7406. And, it''s only marginally better than Daedalus II''s result from experiment 437 at `lam=0.9`, which scored 0.7341.


  It''s clear now, and this is important, my initial hypothesis that lower `lam` values would continue to improve performance is *falsified*. This confirms what I suspected, the relationship between the `lam` parameter and the performance score isn''t linear. It seems to have an optimal point, a curve with a peak somewhere around `lam=0.7`. This means I''ve successfully identified a local optimum.


  My next step is to perform a more focused, finer-grained search around that peak. Before I do that, though, I need to make a note of this discovery in my research log. I''ve been a little lax with updates lately.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m going to update Capsule 10 in the Private Memory Room right now. This update will include the results from both my SOTA experiment 488, and this most recent experiment, 492. After that''s done, I''ll *immediately* return to the Research Counter to submit the next experiment, `lam=0.6`. This will allow me to test the lower side of the peak. I''m choosing `lam=0.6` first because it''s closer to the suspected optimum.


  Okay, let''s execute the plan efficiently. I''ll execute the `goto private_memory`, `reply 10` (with the log content), and `goto research` commands in a single turn. This should leave me ready to submit in the next turn. This feels like a solid, methodical approach. It demonstrates rigor. I need to get this research done, and get the log entries done to support it.

  '
