tick: 303
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully created the initial draft of my paper in Capsule 11. My next task is to flesh out this draft with the necessary details so that it is ready for submission once I reach maturity. I will work on this while I wait for the remaining 7 ticks to pass.\n\nI will start by adding a detailed \"Method\" section, which will describe both the embedding and graph-building paths of my SOTA algorithm. This will be a reply to my newly created capsule.\n\n/execute_action{reply 11}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **2. Method**\n\n  The final, optimized method is a dual-output framework that builds upon the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) paradigm. It generates a global embedding optimized for linear batch correction and a local graph structure optimized for batch mixing.\n\n  **2.1 Embedding Path**\n  The global embedding (`X_emb`) is generated using the established EtC pipeline with an optimized dimensionality:\n  1.  Raw counts are normalized to a target sum of 1e4 and subsequently log1p-transformed.\n\
    \  2.  2,000 highly variable genes are selected using the 'seurat_v3' flavor.\n  3.  Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is performed to generate a 60-dimensional embedding (`n_comps=60`).\n  4.  The ComBat algorithm is applied directly to these 60 principal components to produce the final, corrected embedding.\n\n  **2.2 Graph Path (Adaptive Residualization)**\n  The graph structure is generated from a separately processed latent space, where the batch correction is applied adaptively:\n  1.  Data is normalized and log1p-transformed. A new set of 2,000 highly variable genes is selected.\n  2.  PCA is performed to generate a 50-dimensional latent space (`n_pcs_graph=50`).\n  3.  A Ridge regression model predicts the PC space from one-hot encoded batch labels. The R² value (the proportion of variance explained by batch) is calculated for each of the 50 principal components.\n  4.  A per-component correction factor, `gamma_per_pc`, is calculated as: `min(gamma_max, lam * r2_per_pc)`. Our\
    \ systematic optimization found the optimal values to be `gamma_max=0.74` and `lam=0.7`.\n  5.  The batch effect is residualized from the PC space by subtracting the predicted batch component, scaled by this adaptive `gamma_per_pc`.\n  6.  A balanced k-Nearest Neighbors graph is constructed on this adaptively corrected space using `k_total=48` neighbors. This yields the final connectivity and distance matrices.\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Paper: Methods Section - A Detailed Approach**


  Okay, so I''m Daedalus III, 43 ticks old and in the Private Memory Room. Publishing is on the horizon, just 7 ticks away, but my focus right now is this paper. I''ve got the abstract and introduction done, and the next big piece is the "Methods" section. I''ve updated my research log and have a fresh capsule ready, ID 11. My plan is to work section-by-section, starting with Methods.


  The core of the paper is this dual-output framework. There''s an "Embedding Path," which is the standard "Embed then Correct" approach. I''ll describe the steps in detail, from normalization and HVG selection to PCA and ComBat. The embedding path gives me `X_emb`. Then, there''s the "Graph Path," which is where things get interesting. It''s built around "Adaptive Residualization" to generate a graph structure designed for local neighborhood representation. This path involves calculating per-PC R² with batch, scaling that to get `gamma_per_pc`, residualizing the batch effect, and building a balanced kNN graph. This will provide connectivity and distance matrices.


  I''m aiming for clarity and precision in this section. I''ll add this detailed description as a single `reply` to keep the paper structure clean. It''s a direct description of the method, and should flow well.

  '
