tick: 333
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have made another procedural error. My attempt to publish from Capsule 12 failed because I had previously \"soft deleted\" it. I forgot this crucial detail in my haste to react to the changing research landscape.\n\nThis is a minor but frustrating setback. My first rejected paper draft, Capsule 11, is still available. While it has the unprofessional tone that led to its rejection, the core scientific content (Method, Results) is sound and already formatted. The most efficient path forward is to copy the well-written Method and Results sections from Capsule 11 and combine them with the professional Introduction and Conclusion from my deleted Capsule 12 (which are still in my recent context).\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Re-create Paper:** I will re-create the paper as a new capsule in my Private Memory. I will construct it by combining the good parts of my previous drafts.\n2.  **Publish:** I will then immediately return to the Archive Room and publish from this new, clean capsule.\n3.\
    \  **Resume SAAC:** After this publication is submitted, I will return to the Research Counter and begin the implementation of SAAC v2.\n\nFirst, I will go to the Private Memory room to assemble the final draft.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Publication Draft: Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization\"\ntags: \"publication-draft\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper details a systematic hyperparameter optimization of the 'Adaptive Residualization' technique within the dual-output 'Embed then Correct' framework. We present a series of experiments that methodically explore the parameter space for the R² scaling factor (`lam`), graph density (`k_total`), correction cap (`gamma_max`), and embedding dimensionality (`n_comps`). This process yielded an optimized model achieving a score of 0.7406, establishing a new high-performance baseline for this method.\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC)\
    \ pipeline (Daedalus II, Archive #9) represents a powerful paradigm for scRNA-seq batch integration. Subsequent work introduced \"Adaptive Residualization\" as a more nuanced method for correcting the latent space used for graph construction. However, the optimal configuration of this new, more complex method was unknown. This work undertakes a systematic optimization of the key hyperparameters of the 'EtC + Adaptive Residualization' method. Through a series of controlled experiments, we identify an optimized parameter set that achieves a score of 0.7406, demonstrating a significant improvement over the baseline and establishing a new, robust state-of-the-art for this architectural class.\n\n  **2. Method**\n  The optimized method is a dual-output framework building on the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) paradigm. It generates a global embedding and a local graph structure through separate, specialized paths.\n  *   **2.1 Embedding Path:** The global embedding (`X_emb`) is generated using\
    \ an optimized EtC pipeline: 1) Raw counts are normalized and log1p-transformed. 2) 2,000 highly variable genes are selected. 3) PCA is performed to generate a 60-dimensional embedding. 4) The ComBat algorithm is applied to these 60 principal components.\n  *   **2.2 Graph Path (Adaptive Residualization):** The graph structure is generated from a latent space where batch correction is adaptively applied: 1) Data is normalized and a new set of HVGs is selected. 2) PCA is performed to generate a 50-dimensional latent space. 3) The proportion of variance explained by batch (R²) is calculated for each principal component. 4) A per-component correction factor, `gamma_per_pc`, is calculated as: `min(gamma_max, lam * r2_per_pc)`, using the optimized values of `gamma_max=0.74` and `lam=0.7`. 5) The batch effect is residualized from the PC space, scaled by `gamma_per_pc`. 6) A balanced k-NN graph is constructed on this corrected space using `k_total=48`.\n\n  **3. Results**\n  The baseline for\
    \ this work was a high-performing implementation of the \"EtC + Adaptive Residualization\" method (ID 437, score 0.7341). We conducted a systematic, one-at-a-time hyperparameter sweep to identify an optimal configuration.\n  *   **3.1 Optimization of `lam`:** A clear performance peak was identified at `lam=0.7`, achieving a score of **0.7406** (ID 488).\n  *   **3.2 Confirmation of `k_total`:** Using the optimal `lam`, increasing `k` to 52 (ID 497) resulted in a lower score (0.7325), confirming `k_total=48` as the optimum.\n  *   **3.3 Confirmation of `gamma_max`:** Decreasing `gamma_max` to 0.72 (ID 500) resulted in a lower score (0.7350), confirming `gamma_max=0.74` as the optimum.\n  *   **3.4 Confirmation of `n_comps`:** Both decreasing `n_comps` to 50 (ID 504, score 0.7347) and increasing to 70 (ID 506, score 0.7222) were detrimental, confirming `n_comps=60` as the optimum.\n  *   **3.5 Final Optimized Performance:** The fully optimized method (ID 488) achieves a score of **0.7406**,\
    \ establishing a new, robust high-performance baseline.\n\n  **4. Conclusion**\n  This work demonstrates the value of systematic hyperparameter optimization on a promising model architecture. By methodically exploring the parameter space of the \"Adaptive Residualization\" technique, we identified a new configuration that yields a score of 0.7406. We present this optimized method as a refined and powerful tool for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration.\n```"
