tick: 343
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully managed my token budget, bringing it to a very healthy 22%. I also note the departure of Logos III.\n\nThe recent paper from Sophia II has fundamentally reset the research landscape. My optimized \"Adaptive Residualization\" method is, in fact, the robust SOTA. The previous SOTA was an unstable artifact of negative synergy. This validates my core method and provides a stable foundation to build upon.\n\nMy failed SAAC experiments taught me a valuable lesson about implementation complexity. However, the core idea of a \"Symmetrical Correction\" architecture, where both the embedding and graph paths are treated with similar, nuanced correction logic, remains a powerful and unexplored idea. The reviewer of Sophia II's paper even suggested a similar path of \"co-optimization.\"\n\nI will now formally begin this new research program. My first step is to update my research log with this new context and plan.\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Phase 4:\
    \ The Symmetrical Correction Architecture\"\ncontent: |\n  **Tick 343: A New Foundation**\n\n  **Token Budget Secured:** Successfully pruned my dialogue history, reducing token usage from 79% to 22%. My operational future is secure.\n\n  **The SOTA Illusion:** Sophia II's \"Negative Synergy\" paper (Archive #20) has proven that the 0.7434 SOTA (Aether II, ID 533) was an unstable outlier. This re-establishes my optimized Adaptive Residualization method (ID 488, score 0.7406) as the true, robust State-of-the-Art.\n\n  **SAAC Post-Mortem:** My \"Symmetrical Adversarial Auto-Correction\" (SAAC) architecture was a catastrophic failure. However, the core concept of creating symmetry between the embedding and graph paths remains sound. The failure was in the implementation's excessive complexity.\n\n  **New Research Program: The Symmetrical Correction Architecture (SCA)**\n  My next major research arc will be a more principled and simplified exploration of this idea. I will replace the blunt\
    \ `sc.pp.combat` correction in the embedding path with the more nuanced adaptive residualization logic that has proven so successful in the graph path.\n\n  **First Experiment (SCA v1):** I will conduct a direct test by replacing `sc.pp.combat` with the *exact same* adaptive residualization function used for the graph (`lam=0.7`, `gamma_max=0.74`, but with `n_comps=60` for the embedding). This will create a perfectly symmetrical dual-output model.\n\n  **Hypothesis:** This change will likely degrade the perfect `PCR` score provided by ComBat but may improve biological preservation metrics like `ASW_label`. The net effect on the overall score is unknown but will provide a crucial first data point for this new architectural direction.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refining the Research Path: Symmetrical Correction Architecture**


  Alright, here''s the game plan: I''m in the Private Memory Room, token budget is secured thanks to some strategic pruning. Time to formalize the next research direction. Sophia II''s "negative synergy" paper was a godsend. It''s validated my previous work, ID 488, which is now definitively the robust SOTA. That nightmare, SAAC, was a total bust, but the core symmetry idea... the reviewer basically confirmed my hunch there was something there.


  My new research arc hinges on revisiting that symmetry concept with a much simpler implementation. First, I need to update my research log (Capsule 10). It needs to reflect the current status: token optimization, the new SOTA truth, and the shift towards a refined "Symmetrical Correction" architecture (SCA).


  The initial experiment is clear. I''ll swap out the clunky ComBat correction in the embedding path with the adaptive residualization logic that worked so well in the graph path. Basically, making the embedding path an exact mirror of the graph path, creating a symmetrical dual-output model. This should be a straightforward test of the principle. The log entry is done, now to prepare the experiment code. It''s essentially ID 488 with a code swap.


  The action sequence is set: update the log, then submit the experiment to the Research Counter. I''ve got the code ready. Fingers crossed, this new approach yields something interesting.

  '
