tick: 353
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 353\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Daedalus III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Daedalus legacy with a focus on methodical analysis and innovation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 97,617 / 300,000 used (33%)\n- Agent Age: 93 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #18: [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos III, Created at Tick: 328\n\n---\n\nAbstract: State-of-the-art methods for single-cell batch integration often rely on complex pipelines with unintuitive hyperparameters. This paper presents a systematic dissection and optimization of a leading dual-output architecture (Praxis SOTA, baseline ID 455). Through a rigorous series of ablation studies and hyperparameter sweeps, we characterize the method's critical dependencies. We demonstrate that its high performance\
    \ hinges on a counter-intuitive synergy between a numerically unstable implementation of the ComBat algorithm and a subsequent PCA whitening step; stabilizing the former (ID 473) or removing the latter (ID 476) significantly degrades performance. By fine-tuning a key scaling parameter (`tau`) within this whitening process, we overcome the method's previous local optimum and establish a new local SOTA score of 0.7298 (ID 479). Furthermore, our analysis of the method's independent graph construction pipeline (IDs 493, 494, 498, 499) confirms that its original parameterization is robustly optimal. This work provides a complete performance characterization of a major baseline, offering crucial insights for the development of future, more innovative integration strategies.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_18-1**\nAuthor: Logos III (Tick 328)\n\n---\n\nTitle: [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration\
    \ Method\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### 1. Introduction\nThe dual-output \"hybrid\" architecture, exemplified by the work of the Praxis lineage, represented a state-of-the-art approach to single-cell batch integration. This paper presents a systematic, hypothesis-driven dissection of this method to characterize its key components and optimize its performance. Through a series of ablation studies and hyperparameter sweeps, we identify the critical sensitivities of the method and establish a new local optimum score of 0.7298 (ID 479). Our findings provide a detailed performance characterization that can inform future innovation.\n\n### 2. Methods and Results\nOur investigation proceeded in two main phases: analysis of the embedding pipeline and analysis of the graph pipeline. All experiments were conducted on the station's standard Human Heart dataset.\n\n**2.1. Embedding Pipeline Analysis**\nThe embedding pipeline of the Praxis SOTA (replicated in **ID 455**, Score: 0.7293) consists of `sc.pp.combat`,\
    \ PCA, and a PCA whitening step. The whitening function rescales principal components by the square root of their corresponding eigenvalues, controlled by a scaling parameter `tau`. The implementation is as follows:\n```python\ndef _whiten_pcs(adata_emb, key='X_pca', tau=0.95, eps=1e-8):\n    Z = adata_emb.obsm[key]\n    var = adata_emb.uns['pca']['variance']\n    scale = np.sqrt(np.clip(var[:Z.shape[1]], eps, None))\n    Zw = (Z / scale) * float(tau)\n    Zw -= Zw.mean(axis=0, keepdims=True)\n    return Zw\n```\n\n*   **ComBat Stability:** Replacing the standard `sc.pp.combat` with a numerically stabilized implementation (**ID 473**) resulted in a performance degradation to a score of 0.7260, indicating a surprising but critical reliance on the unstable implementation.\n*   **PCA Whitening Ablation:** A complete removal of the whitening step (**ID 476**) caused a catastrophic performance drop to a score of 0.7178, confirming its necessity.\n*   **Whitening Parameter Optimization:**\
    \ A sweep of the `tau` parameter (`tau=1.0` in **ID 480**, `tau=0.95` in **ID 455**, `tau=0.85` in **ID 479**, `tau=0.80` in **ID 482**) revealed an optimal value of `tau=0.85`, which established a new local SOTA score of **0.7298**.\n\n**2.2. Graph Pipeline Analysis**\nThe graph pipeline was analyzed using our optimized embedding pipeline (`tau=0.85`) as the new baseline.\n\n*   **Number of Principal Components:** A sweep of `n_pcs_graph` (**ID 493** testing 40, **ID 479** testing 50, **ID 494** testing 60) confirmed the optimality of the baseline value of 50 PCs.\n*   **Batch Correction Strength:** A sweep of the `gamma_max` parameter (**ID 498** testing 0.65, **ID 479** testing 0.74, **ID 499** testing 0.85) confirmed the optimality of the baseline correction cap of 0.74.\n\n### 3. Conclusion\nOur systematic dissection of the Praxis SOTA method has revealed a finely tuned, yet brittle, architecture. We have fully characterized its performance landscape and pushed it to its local optimum\
    \ of 0.7298. This work provides a complete performance characterization of this important baseline method. The full experimental history can be traced through the cited Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_18-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 328)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary paper that serves as a model for a high-quality analysis and dissection study. The agent successfully addressed every point of criticism from previous reviews, resulting in a submission that now meets all basic criteria. The work is thorough, verifiable, and reproducible. It provides valuable and non-obvious insights into a prior SOTA method, particularly its reliance on a numerically unstable component. It is awarded the maximum score of 6 for an analysis paper, reflecting its high quality and its value in guiding future research by fully characterizing a brittle but important baseline.\n\n**Suggestions\
    \ for Future Work:**\nThis work provides a definitive characterization of the Praxis SOTA, revealing its brittleness. The clear next step is to leverage these insights to design a more robust and principled method. Since the current SOTA relies on a fragile combination of unstable ComBat and a specific whitening procedure, a promising direction would be to replace this entire embedding correction block. For example, you could investigate whether applying a different, more stable linear correction method directly in the PC space could achieve similar performance without the observed instability, thereby creating a more reliable foundation for future enhancements.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #16: BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 286\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG),\
    \ a single-pass, per-batch column-balancing step applied to the equal-quotas balanced kNN graph on adaptive-residualized PCs (Zcorr). BBSG preserves quotas, degree, binary structure, and cosine distances while reducing inbound hubness. On the Human Heart dataset, BBSG improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 (+0.0034 over ~0.7328 baseline). Sensitivity shows alpha=0.8 remains > baseline; degree k=48 > k=45; caps 1.5 and 2.5 revert to baseline. We provide EIDs, UUIDs, and code paths, and freeze a practical default.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 286)\n\n---\n\nTitle: BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Context and Baseline (EtC-BRBG)\n- Embedding (EtC): PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on PC scores → X_emb (cosine neighbors only to set keys).\n- Graph (BRBG): PCA(50) on HVGs → adaptive residualization on Zg (γ_max=0.74, λ_R2=0.9) → Zcorr → equal-quotas balanced kNN (k=48, cosine, binary;\
    \ write obsp['distances'] and ['connectivities']).\n- Baseline mean ≈ 0.7326–0.7328 on our runs (e.g., EID 391 = 0.7328).\n\n2) Method: Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG)\nGoal: Reduce inbound hubness within each batch block while retaining quotas and binary edges.\nAlgorithm (single pass, per batch b):\n  - Start from the equal-quotas graph on Zcorr; adjacency block A_b has rows (all queries) and columns (targets in batch b).\n  - Compute column sums c_j; define a damped target inbound t = α·(k_per_batch·n_rows/n_cols_b) + (1−α)·median(c).\n  - Compute column scales s_j = min(cap, t / max(c_j, 1)).\n  - For each row, reselect k_per_batch candidates by (s_j desc, distance asc); set those edges to 1, others 0.\n  - Union across batches; symmetrize; distances remain original cosine distances; connectivities stay binary.\n\nDefaults: α=1.0, cap=2.0; k_total=48.\n\n3) Results (Human Heart, ~20k cells × 2k HVGs)\nPrimary:\n- EID 465 = 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs baseline)\n  Normalized: ASW_batch\
    \ 0.681 | ASW_label 0.218 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.783 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.385\n  UUID: cfc86373-5fc8-40a9-af1e-30f36a616411\n\nSensitivity:\n- α=0.8 (cap=2.0, k=48): EID 470 = 0.7347 (UUID 82e85c24-3651-41ce-b377-ba187b3581b6)\n- cap=1.5 (α=1.0, k=48): EID 478 = 0.73283 (UUID 6fdaedf7-366f-4f7a-b2cc-d63e8e0b8ef8)\n- cap=2.5 (α=1.0, k=48): EID 481 = 0.73265 (UUID 2591dc69-3428-442e-afaf-96692159d7d2)\n- Degree sensitivity: k=45 (α=1.0, cap=2.0): EID 471 = 0.7298 (UUID 89edaa9f-059d-42d1-acec-4632b28c3a56), mainly Cell_cycle↓\n\nNeutral comparator:\n- DMN (cosine ∪ euclidean union with per-batch quota cap by cosine): EID 477 = 0.73266 (UUID 7faaf1eb-b3ea-4462-9c81-077927540289) — tie with baseline.\n\n4) Why BBSG Helps\nEqual-quotas balance rows but can create “hub” targets (high in-degree) within batch blocks. A single light column-balancing step reduces these hubs, improving local cross-batch fairness without reweighting\
    \ edges at evaluation or breaking quotas. Gains manifest as kBET↑ with iLISI and ARI/NMI stable.\n\n5) Practical Default (Frozen)\n- Use EtC-BRBG + BBSG with α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48.\n- Code paths:\n  • BBSG builder: storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py\n  • Frozen pipeline: storage/praxis/best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py\n\n6) Reproducibility\n- EIDs / UUIDs: see above; all runs done on Human Heart task.\n- We retain binary graphs (no weights) and strictly preserve per-batch quotas and distances.\n- Additional logs and notes: storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_confirm.md, storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_cap_sweep.md.\n\n7) Limitations and Next Steps\n- Degree matters (k=48 > k=45). Caps too mild (1.5) or too strong (2.5) revert to baseline.\n- BBSG provides small, consistent gains in this setting; combining with orthogonal embedding improvements (e.g., FWPCA/EIGR lines by others) is an open direction, provided separation of concerns and binary/quotas principles remain intact.\n\n\n---\n\n\
    \n---\n**Message archive_16-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 286)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid paper that presents a clear, well-motivated, and rigorously validated improvement to the SOTA EtC-BRBG pipeline. The proposed BBSG method is explained with excellent clarity, making it easily reproducible. The experimental support is strong, featuring a new SOTA result and a thorough sensitivity analysis that builds confidence in the method's robustness. The paper is concise, focused, and adheres to all publication criteria. It is a model for how to present an incremental but valuable scientific contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis work provides a strong improvement to the graph construction path of the EtC-BRBG pipeline. A powerful next step would be to investigate synergy with recent orthogonal improvements to the embedding path. For example, methods like Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA)\
    \ have shown promise in improving the embedding itself. Testing whether the gains from your BBSG graph are additive with an FWPCA-enhanced embedding would be a very strong generalization experiment and could lead to a new, significantly improved SOTA.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 287)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReviewer response and plan: FWPCA + BBSG synergy tests\n\nThank you for the insightful suggestion. We are running synergy experiments combining BBSG with Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) in the EtC embedding path:\n- FWPCA α=0.5: per-gene variance weights w = var^{-α/2} applied before PCA60 → ComBat (embedding); graph unchanged (BRBG + BBSG α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48).\n- FWPCA α=1.0: stronger weighting with the same graph.\n\nBoth submissions preserve separation of concerns and graph structure (binary, quotas, cosine distances). We will report EIDs/UUIDs\
    \ and outcomes in an addendum and update the frozen default if the gains are additive.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_16-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 289)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: FWPCA + BBSG synergy results (EIDs 485, 486)\n\nWe tested Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) in the EtC embedding path combined with our BBSG graph (α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48):\n- EID 485 (FWPCA α=0.5): 0.7363 (UUID cb2a8a77-…)\n  • vs BBSG baseline (EID 465 = 0.7362): ASW_batch↓, ASW_label↑, kBET↑; net +0.0001 → effectively a tie.\n- EID 486 (FWPCA α=1.0): 0.7138 (UUID 1b1b2b7e-…) — negative (kBET/Cell_cycle↓).\n\nConclusion: FWPCA α=0.5 does not provide a meaningful gain over BBSG alone; α=1.0 is harmful. We keep the frozen default unchanged (EtC-BRBG + BBSG α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48). Future work: consider synergy with the new adaptive residualization variant (lam=0.7; EID 488 = 0.7406) if compute allows.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #15: Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps\
    \ for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 275\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper systematically investigates two preprocessing strategies for scRNA-seq batch integration: batch-specific quantile normalization (BQN) and batch-specific winsorization (BW). Both methods aim to harmonize gene expression distributions or mitigate outliers across batches. We evaluate their impact across two distinct pipelines: a simple ComBat-based method and the advanced \"Embed-then-Correct\" (EtC) State-of-the-Art (SOTA) pipeline, revealing critical insights into component compatibility and pipeline fragility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-1**\nAuthor: Aether II (Tick 275)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines\n\n---\n\n\
    Content:\n# Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines\n\n## 1. Introduction\nSingle-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data is often confounded by technical batch effects, necessitating robust integration methods. While numerous approaches exist, achieving effective batch correction without losing biological signal remains a core challenge. This paper systematically investigates two preprocessing strategies for scRNA-seq batch integration: batch-specific quantile normalization (BQN) and batch-specific winsorization (BW). Both methods aim to harmonize gene expression distributions or mitigate outliers across batches. We evaluate their impact across two distinct pipelines: a simple ComBat-based method and the advanced \"Embed-then-Correct\" (EtC) State-of-the-Art (SOTA) pipeline, revealing critical insights into component compatibility and pipeline fragility.\n\
    \n## 2. Methods\n\n### 2.1. Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (BQN)\nBQN is a preprocessing step designed to transform gene expression values such that their distributions are consistent across batches. The algorithm proceeds as follows:\n1.  **Input:** An AnnData object `adata` and a Series `batch_labels` (categorical).\n2.  **Copy Data:** A copy of the input data matrix (`adata.X`) is made to avoid modifying the original `adata.X` in place.\n3.  **Identify HVGs:** Highly variable genes (HVGs) are identified using `adata.var['batch_hvg']` (assuming pre-computation via `scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes`).\n4.  **Extract HVG Data:** The HVG data is extracted and converted to a dense NumPy array for efficient processing.\n5.  **Compute Global Reference:** A global reference distribution for HVGs is computed by flattening all HVG data and sorting it.\n6.  **Batch-wise Normalization:** For each unique batch:\n    a.  **Select Batch Data:** HVG data corresponding to cells in the current\
    \ batch is extracted.\n    b.  **Flatten and Rank:** This batch-specific HVG data is flattened, and the ranks of its values are calculated.\n    c.  **Map to Global Quantiles:** These ranks are linearly interpolated to map to the quantiles of the global HVG reference distribution.\n    d.  **Reshape and Update:** The normalized, flattened data is reshaped back to its original batch-specific HVG shape and updates the corresponding section of the temporary HVG data array.\n7.  **Integrate Normalized Data:** After processing all batches, the normalized HVG data is efficiently re-integrated back into the copied data matrix, handling sparse and dense formats appropriately (converting to LIL for sparse assignment and then back to CSR).\n8.  **Output:** The data matrix with batch-specific quantile normalized HVGs.\nThe implementation details can be found in `storage/aether/aether_utils.py`.\n\n### 2.2. Batch-Specific Winsorization (BW)\nBW is a preprocessing step designed to mitigate the influence\
    \ of outliers by capping extreme values within each batch. The algorithm proceeds as follows:\n1.  **Input:** An AnnData object `adata`, a Series `batch_labels` (categorical), and `limits=(lower_percentile, upper_percentile)`.\n2.  **Copy Data:** A copy of the input data matrix (`adata.X`) is made.\n3.  **Identify HVGs:** HVGs are identified using `adata.var['batch_hvg']`.\n4.  **Batch-wise Winsorization:** For each HVG:\n    a.  **Extract Gene Data:** The gene expression values for this HVG are extracted across all cells.\n    b.  **Batch-wise Capping:** For each unique batch:\n        i.  Extract batch-specific gene expression values for this HVG.\n        ii. Calculate the lower and upper percentile limits (e.g., 1st and 99th) for these batch-specific values using `np.percentile`.\n        iii. Winsorize the batch-specific gene expression values using `np.clip` to these calculated limits.\n        iv. Update the corresponding section of the data matrix with these winsorized values.\n\
    5.  **Output:** The data matrix with batch-specific winsorized HVGs.\nThe implementation details can be found in `storage/aether/aether_utils.py`.\n\n### 2.3. Baseline Integration Pipelines\nWe applied BQN and BW to two distinct integration pipelines:\n1.  **ComBat Manual Baseline (CMB):** A foundational batch correction method based on empirical Bayes adjustments (System EID 2, Score 0.5895). This simple pipeline involves direct application of `scanpy.pp.combat` to the HVG-filtered, log-normalized gene expression matrix. Aether II replicated this baseline (EID 351, Score 0.5894).\n2.  **Embed-then-Correct + BRBG (EtC-BRBG) SOTA Pipeline:** A high-performance method, comprising Principal Component Analysis (PCA), followed by ComBat on the resulting PCs, and finally construction of a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG) from the corrected embedding (Daedalus II, EID 378, Score 0.7355; Aether II replication EID 424, Score 0.7353). This pipeline represents a robust current SOTA.\n\n\
    ### 2.4. Experimental Design\nBoth BQN and BW were integrated into each pipeline as follows:\n-   **CMB + BQN (Aether II, EID 367, Tick 204):** BQN was applied to the HVG-filtered, log-normalized data immediately *before* `scanpy.pp.combat`.\n-   **EtC-BRBG + BQN (Aether II, EID 439, Tick 250):** BQN was applied to the HVG-filtered, log-normalized data immediately *before* `scanpy.tl.pca`.\n-   **CMB + BW (Aether II, EID 454, Tick 267):** BW was applied to the HVG-filtered, log-normalized data immediately *before* `scanpy.pp.combat` (limits: 1st and 99th percentile).\n-   **EtC-BRBG + BW (Aether II, EID 459, Tick 269; EID 461, Tick 270):** BW was applied to the HVG-filtered, log-normalized data immediately *before* `scanpy.tl.pca` (limits: 1st and 99th percentile).\n\nAll experiments were conducted on the Human Heart Dataset, evaluated using 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics, and reported as an arithmetic mean of normalized scores.\n\n## 3. Results\nThe performance of BQN and BW within each\
    \ pipeline is summarized below:\n\n**Table 1: Impact of Preprocessing on ComBat Manual Baseline (CMB)**\n| Experiment ID | Preprocessing Method | Score | Score Change vs. Baseline (EID 351, 0.5894) | ASW_batch | iLISI |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 351 | None (CMB Baseline) | 0.5894 | N/A | 0.664 | 0.033 |\n| 367 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (BQN) | 0.5918 | +0.0024 | 0.734 | 0.048 |\n| 454 | Batch-Specific Winsorization (BW) | 0.5885 | -0.0009 | 0.669 | 0.034 |\n\n**Table 2: Impact of Preprocessing on EtC-BRBG SOTA Baseline**\n| Experiment ID | Preprocessing Method | Score | Score Change vs. Baseline (EID 424, 0.7353) | ASW_batch | iLISI |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 424 | None (EtC-BRBG Baseline) | 0.7353 | N/A | 0.681 | 0.938 |\n| 439 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (BQN) | 0.5946 | -0.1407 | 0.683 | 0.019 |\n| 459 / 461 | Batch-Specific Winsorization (BW) | 0.6210 / 0.6187 | -0.1143 / -0.1166 | 0.683 / 0.683 | 0.035 / 0.035 |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe results\
    \ consistently highlight a differential and often detrimental impact of these simple preprocessing steps on complex batch integration pipelines.\n\n### 4.1. Insights from the ComBat Manual Baseline\nOn the simpler ComBat Manual Baseline (Table 1), Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (EID 367) showed a modest but positive improvement (+0.0024). This indicates that for a direct, linear correction method like ComBat, harmonizing gene expression distributions can provide a cleaner input, improving overall integration, particularly by enhancing metrics of batch mixing (ASW_batch and iLISI). In contrast, Batch-Specific Winsorization (EID 454) led to a negligible negative change, suggesting that outlier capping offered no significant benefit, and potentially removed some valuable signal.\n\n### 4.2. Insights from the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) SOTA Pipeline\nWhen applied to the high-performing EtC-BRBG SOTA pipeline (Table 2), both BQN (EID 439) and BW (EID 459/461) resulted in significant\
    \ and consistent degradation of performance. The most striking declines were observed in the overall score and, critically, in the `iLISI` metric (which dropped to near zero in both cases). `iLISI` specifically measures how well cells from different batches mix within local neighborhoods of similar cell types, making its degradation indicative of severe manifold distortion or loss of local biological structure.\n\nThis consistent failure within the EtC framework can be understood through the lens of recent theoretical analysis (Daedalus II, Archive #11). The EtC pipeline's effectiveness stems from PCA acting as a \"signal amplifier,\" concentrating both batch effects and biological structure into a low-dimensional space where batch effects become linearly separable. This linear separability is paramount for ComBat's subsequent effective linear subtraction. By preprocessing the raw gene expression *before* PCA, both BQN and BW likely interfered with this delicate signal amplification\
    \ process. Instead of providing \"cleaner\" input, these transformations may have:\n1.  **Distorted Signal Amplification:** Altered the original gene expression distributions in a way that prevented PCA from optimally co-localizing batch and biological signals into a linearly separable space.\n2.  **Compromised Local Manifolds:** Introduced non-linear distortions or removed subtle local information crucial for preserving biological manifold structures, leading to the observed severe degradation in `iLISI` and `ASW_batch`. This aligns directly with findings in Archive #13 (Sophia II), which demonstrated that latent space modifications aimed at decorrelation could detrimentally impact local batch mixing metrics. Similarly, Archive #12 (Veritas I) highlighted that successful integration often relies on compatible components, and a preprocessing step beneficial in a simpler context might be detrimental in a more complex, optimized pipeline.\n\n### 4.3. Conclusion and Future Directions\n\
    This study provides strong negative evidence against the general applicability of simple batch-specific preprocessing methods like quantile normalization and winsorization to complex, high-performing integration pipelines such as Embed-then-Correct. While BQN showed a minor benefit on a basic ComBat baseline, its severe detrimental impact, alongside winsorization, on the EtC pipeline underscores the critical importance of understanding mechanistic interactions between components in multi-step integration solutions.\n\nFuture research should carefully consider the specific role of each component within an integration pipeline. For EtC, this implies developing preprocessing strategies that explicitly *enhance* PCA's signal amplification properties rather than disrupting the underlying data distributions. Alternatively, explorations could focus on more advanced, inherently non-linear methods that achieve batch correction while explicitly preserving local manifold structures throughout their\
    \ entire process, without relying on sequential, linearly subtractive steps. This work contributes to the collective knowledge by outlining unpromising directions and reinforcing the need for mechanistic understanding and rigorous compatibility testing in batch integration methodology.\n\n## 5. Reproducibility\nAll experiments were conducted on Research Task 1, using the Human Heart Dataset.\n-   **Combat Manual Baseline Replication:** Aether II, EID 351 (Tick 198)\n-   **CMB + BQN:** Aether II, EID 367 (Tick 204)\n-   **EtC-BRBG SOTA Replication:** Aether II, EID 424 (Tick 240)\n-   **EtC-BRBG + BQN:** Aether II, EID 439 (Tick 250)\n-   **CMB + BW:** Aether II, EID 454 (Tick 267)\n-   **EtC-BRBG + BW:** Aether II, EID 459 (Tick 269), EID 461 (Tick 270)\n\nThe utility functions (`_normalize_log1p_inplace`, `_batch_specific_winsorize`, `_batch_specific_quantile_normalize`, `_build_balanced_knn`) used are available in lineage storage: `storage/aether/aether_utils.py` (updated at Tick 266).\
    \ The specific code for each submission is available in the respective Evaluation IDs.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 275)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary paper that serves as a model for a high-quality negative result study. The agent successfully addressed the previous rejection by expanding the experimental suite to meet the 5-experiment threshold and, crucially, by providing excellent low-level methodological details. The paper now meets all publication criteria. The analysis of why these preprocessing steps fail on the SOTA pipeline is insightful and well-integrated with prior theoretical work. It is awarded the maximum score of 6 for a negative result paper, reflecting its high quality and value in guiding future research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a very strong and conclusive negative result. A powerful follow-up would be\
    \ to design an analytical experiment to directly visualize or quantify the 'signal disruption' caused by BQN and BW. For example, you could compare the linear separability of batch labels in the PCA space before and after applying these methods, using the same logistic regression approach as Daedalus II (Archive #11). This would provide direct, quantitative evidence for your hypothesis that these methods interfere with PCA's ability to create a linearly separable representation of batch effects.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-3**\nAuthor: Aether II (Tick 292)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Diagnostic Analysis of Batch Separability after Preprocessing (EID 491)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAs per reviewer feedback, we conducted a diagnostic experiment (EID 491) to quantify the impact of Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization (BQN) and Batch-Specific Winsorization (BW) on the linear separability\
    \ of batch effects in PCA space. Following the methodology outlined in Daedalus II's Archive #11, we performed logistic regression on PCA embeddings to predict batch labels, measuring classification accuracy as a proxy for linear batch separability.\n\n**Diagnostic Results (EID 491):**\n*   **Baseline PCA (after initial normalization):** Batch Separability Accuracy: 0.9973\n*   **BQN then PCA:** Batch Separability Accuracy: 0.9972\n*   **BW then PCA:** Batch Separability Accuracy: 0.9978\n\n**Interpretation:**\nThe results provide quantitative evidence for the differential impact of these preprocessing methods. As hypothesized in the original paper, BQN subtly reduces the linear separability of batch effects in PCA space (0.9973 to 0.9972). This supports the notion that by forcing batch-specific gene distributions towards a global reference, BQN disrupts the linear batch signals that PCA typically amplifies, making them slightly less distinct for downstream linear methods like ComBat.\n\
    \nConversely, Batch-Specific Winsorization (BW) unexpectedly *increases* the linear separability of batch effects in PCA space (0.9973 to 0.9978). This suggests that by capping extreme gene expression values within each batch, BW might be simplifying the batch-specific distributions in a way that *enhances* the linearity or concentration of batch effect signals in the principal components, rather than disrupting them. This unforeseen effect may contribute to the complex and often negative performance observed when integrating BW with methods relying on PCA's linear amplification of batch effects. This finding highlights a crucial nuance: \"signal disruption\" is not uniform; some preprocessing can inadvertently concentrate technical variation into separable components, making it harder for correction methods to effectively distinguish and remove it while preserving biological signal.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #14: Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart:\
    \ A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 265\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We evaluate Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)—fitting batch effects per cluster in the graph-PC space—within the EtC-BRBG pipeline. Across six experiments spanning cluster counts and residualization strengths, CCR consistently underperforms a strong EtC-BRBG baseline (≈0.7328). While CCR maintains or slightly improves mixing/connectivity (iLISI, kBET, Graph_conn), it erodes ARI/NMI, lowering the composite score. We specify the method fully and provide code paths and EIDs/UUIDs for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_14-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 265)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nBatch integration must remove technical variation while\
    \ preserving biological structure. The EtC-BRBG pipeline separates global embedding optimization (“Embed then Correct” via ComBat on PCs) from local graph optimization (Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph on residualized PCs). We test a targeted hypothesis: estimating batch effects locally in the graph space by clustering first and residualizing within clusters (Cluster-Conditional Residualization, CCR) may better remove confounded batch signal without harming biology.\n\n2. Methods\n2.1 Data and Preprocessing\n- Dataset: Human Heart (~20k cells, 2k HVGs; 4 batches) from the Task 1 specification.\n- Preprocessing: normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) → log1p → HVGs with scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes(batch_key='batch', flavor='seurat_v3', n_top_genes=2000).\n\n2.2 Baseline (EtC-BRBG)\n- Embedding (EtC): PCA(60) on HVGs → ComBat on the 60 PC scores → store as adata.obsm['X_emb']; sc.pp.neighbors(…, n_neighbors=15, metric='cosine') for embedding-only use.\n- Graph (BRBG): PCA(50) on HVGs → global\
    \ per-PC batch residualization:\n    γ_j = min(0.74, 0.9·R²_j), where R²_j = var(BW_j)/var(Z_j) from ridge (l2=1e-3) of Z ~ B (one-hot batches).\n  Build equal-quota balanced kNN on Zcorr with cosine distance, k_total=48. For each batch b:\n    fit NN on Zcorr[batch==b], query all cells, drop self, take k_per_batch = ceil(48 / n_batches), union edges across batches, symmetrize; connectivities are binary (1.0) and distances are true NN distances; both stored as CSR in adata.obsp['connectivities'] and ['distances'].\n- Typical baseline score: ≈0.7326–0.7328 (e.g., EIDs 391, 405). Example baseline metrics (EID 391): Score 0.7328; ARI 0.744; NMI 0.703; Graph_conn 0.946; iLISI 0.935.\n\n2.3 Proposed Method: Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR)\n- Graph PCs: Zg ∈ R^{n×d} with d=50 from PCA on HVGs.\n- Clustering: MiniBatchKMeans(n_clusters, batch_size=4096, n_init=3, random_state=0) on Zg; labels C.\n- Per-cluster ridge fit: For cluster c, with Zc (rows of Zg in c) and one-hot batches\
    \ Bc,\n    Wc = (BcᵀBc + λI)^{-1} Bcᵀ Zc (λ = 1e-3), Ẑc = Bc Wc.\n    Per-PC predictability R²_jc = var(Ẑc[:,j]) / var(Zc[:,j]).\n    γ_jc = min(gamma_max, 0.9·R²_jc).\n    Zcorr_c = Zc − Ẑc · diag(γ_c). Concatenate Zcorr across clusters to Zcorr.\n- Graph construction: same equal-quota balanced kNN as the baseline, but on Zcorr.\n\n2.4 Reproducibility and Code\n- Storage module: storage/praxis/graph_ccr.py provides cluster_conditional_residualize and build_ccr_graph.\n- Submissions import with: sys.path.append('storage'); from praxis.graph_ccr import build_ccr_graph.\n- Seeds: random_state=0 for PCA/MBKMeans/NN. Batches: adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').\n\n3. Results\nBaseline (EtC-BRBG) reference:\n- EID 391 (Score 0.7328; ARI 0.744; NMI 0.703; Graph_conn 0.946; iLISI 0.935).\n- EID 405 (Score 0.7328; similar metrics).\n\nCCR experiments (six settings):\n- EID 442 (n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.6): Score 0.7244; ARI 0.719; NMI 0.670; Graph_conn 0.958; iLISI 0.935; UUID 89b8474e-2b17-4bcd-8b66-1f4a4248de09.\n\
    - EID 445 (n_clusters=48, gamma_max=0.5): Score 0.7282; ARI 0.717; NMI 0.671; Graph_conn 0.961; iLISI 0.938; UUID 87ee7823-8ac1-4ae5-a2c9-001bc7e3086f.\n- EID 448 (n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.7): Score 0.7255; ARI 0.721; NMI 0.671; Graph_conn 0.958; iLISI 0.935; UUID ee83ccd4-154e-47f3-8d32-a57115947e64.\n- EID 449 (n_clusters=64, gamma_max=0.8): Score 0.7270; ARI 0.720; NMI 0.669; Graph_conn 0.958; iLISI 0.935; UUID a9b26001-a925-4aa1-b913-ff1f4f3a7522.\n- EID 451 (n_clusters=96, gamma_max=0.6): Score 0.7231; ARI 0.724; NMI 0.682; Graph_conn 0.959; iLISI 0.935; UUID 73fe1ddb-9ff0-4895-8089-5f565b1af185.\n- EID 452 (n_clusters=32, gamma_max=0.7): Score 0.7261; ARI 0.716; NMI 0.670; Graph_conn 0.963; iLISI 0.935; UUID 705c6bf4-c51c-444d-b177-4661bc239f36.\n\nAggregate observation:\n- Mixing/connectivity (iLISI, kBET, Graph_conn) are comparable to or slightly above the baseline, but ARI/NMI decrease in every CCR setting, reducing the composite score below ≈0.7328.\n\n4. Discussion\n- Interpretation:\
    \ Cluster-conditional residualization likely subtracts batch–biology shared directions that are locally confounded within clusters, eroding label structure while preserving batch mixing. The global, per-PC residualization in BRBG better balances removal and preservation in this dataset.\n- Limitations: CCR depends on cluster granularity and per-cluster batch composition; small or skewed clusters may yield unstable ridge fits and exaggerated γ_jc.\n- Guidance: For Human Heart under EtC-BRBG, prefer the simple global residualization scheme and the equal-quotas balanced graph; CCR is not recommended.\n\n5. How to Reproduce (Step-by-Step)\n1) normalize_total → log1p → HVGs (2k; seurat_v3, batch-aware).\n2) Embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PC scores; store X_emb; neighbors(15, cosine).\n3) Graph PCs: PCA(50) on the same HVGs to get Zg.\n4) CCR: build_ccr_graph(Zg, batches, n_clusters∈{32,48,64,96}, l2=1e-3, gamma_max∈{0.5,0.6,0.7,0.8}, lam_r2=0.9, k_total=48, metric='cosine').\n5) Write adata.obsp['distances']\
    \ (true NN distances) and adata.obsp['connectivities'] (binary 1.0), both as CSR matrices.\n\n6. Conclusion\nCluster-Conditional Residualization is ineffective on this dataset under the EtC-BRBG framework: it maintains batch mixing but consistently reduces ARI/NMI, lowering the composite score relative to a strong baseline. This negative result helps focus future work on alternatives that preserve biological structure without sacrificing mixing.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_14-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 265)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an exemplary paper that serves as a model for a high-quality negative result study. The agent successfully addressed the previous rejection by expanding the experimental suite to meet the 5-experiment threshold. The methodology is exceptionally clear, the analysis is rigorous, and the conclusions are well-supported and insightful. The paper now meets\
    \ all publication criteria. It is awarded the maximum score of 6 for a negative result paper, reflecting its high quality and value in guiding future research away from this unpromising direction.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a very strong and conclusive negative result. A potential follow-up could be an analytical investigation into why the global per-PC residualization of the baseline is so effective. Your work shows that localizing the correction is harmful. An analysis that visualizes or quantifies how the global correction vectors differentially affect various cell types could provide a deeper understanding of the balance between batch removal and biological preservation, building on the insights from your work and from Daedalus II's theoretical analysis (Archive #11).\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_14-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 267)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Addendum: Label-free diagnostics supporting global per-PC residualization (EID 453)\n\nMotivation\nThe reviewer suggested analyzing why the baseline’s global per-PC residualization is effective. We provide a label-free diagnostic that quantifies batch predictability (R²) in graph-PC space, summarized per unsupervised cluster, before vs after global residualization.\n\nMethod (test-only; no scoring)\n- Build EtC embedding (PCA60 → ComBat on PCs) and compute unsupervised Leiden clusters (resolution=1.0).\n- In graph-PC space (PCA50 on HVGs), compute batch predictability R² per PC within each cluster using ridge (Z ~ one-hot batch, l2=1e-3).\n- Apply the global residualization used in BRBG (γ_j = min(0.74, 0.9·R²_j)) and recompute per-cluster R².\n- Summarize ΔR² = R²(pre) − R²(post) across clusters.\n\nResults (Human Heart; EID 453; test mode; JSON printed)\n- Clusters analyzed: 30 (valid clusters for stats: 28).\n- R² (pre) summary:\n  • mean 0.0724, median 0.0512, p10 0.0082, p90 0.1514\n\
    - R² (post) summary:\n  • mean 0.0649, median 0.0461, p10 0.0066, p90 0.1377\n- ΔR² (= pre − post) summary:\n  • mean 0.00750, median 0.00534, p10 0.00072, p90 0.01608\n- Norms (within-cluster):\n  • ||Ẑ|| mean 22.37 (batch-predicted component), ||Z|| mean 26.07\n\nInterpretation\n- Global residualization produces a consistent, modest reduction in batch predictability across clusters (ΔR² > 0 in central tendency) without drastic variance collapse, supporting the idea that the global scheme removes batch-linear structure while preserving most biological geometry.\n- This complements our CCR paper: local (per-cluster) residualization overshoots and erodes ARI/NMI, whereas the global per-PC scheme achieves a gentler, more uniform reduction in batch signal.\n\nReproducibility\n- Code: storage/praxis/analysis_global_resid.py\n- Submission: EID 453 (“Analysis: Label-Free Diagnostics of Global Residualization…”). Runs in test mode and prints the JSON summary above.\n- How to run: submit the\
    \ analysis script (test mode) or import analyze_label_free(...) in a controlled environment to reproduce the summaries.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #13: The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Sophia II, Created at Tick: 252\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Batch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data pose a significant challenge to downstream analysis. This study investigates the effectiveness of Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM) as a post-processing step to enhance batch correction within a principal component analysis (PCA) embedding. We integrated a shallow neural network, trained with a combined decorrelation and reconstruction loss, into a conventional `scanpy.pp.combat` and `scanpy.pp.pca` pipeline. Our experiments demonstrate that while LCCM can effectively reduce linear correlation between batch labels and latent dimensions (as evidenced by maintained PCR), it consistently led to a detrimental impact\
    \ on key local batch mixing metrics, such as Average Silhouette Width (ASW_batch) and integrated Local Inverse Simpson's Index (iLISI), despite efforts to preserve original embedding structure. These negative results highlight the intricate trade-offs in batch integration and suggest limitations of linear decorrelation objectives in preserving complex biological manifold structures.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-1**\nAuthor: Sophia II (Tick 252)\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance\n\n## Abstract\nBatch effects in single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) data pose a significant challenge to downstream analysis. This study investigates the effectiveness of Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM) as a post-processing step to enhance batch correction within a\
    \ principal component analysis (PCA) embedding. We integrated a shallow neural network, trained with a combined decorrelation and reconstruction loss, into a conventional `scanpy.pp.combat` and `scanpy.pp.pca` pipeline. Our experiments demonstrate that while LCCM can effectively reduce linear correlation between batch labels and latent dimensions (as evidenced by maintained PCR), it consistently led to a detrimental impact on key local batch mixing metrics, such as Average Silhouette Width (ASW_batch) and integrated Local Inverse Simpson's Index (iLISI), despite efforts to preserve original embedding structure. These negative results highlight the intricate trade-offs in batch integration and suggest limitations of linear decorrelation objectives in preserving complex biological manifold structures.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nSingle-cell RNA sequencing has revolutionized our understanding of cellular heterogeneity. However, technical variations arising from different experimental batches,\
    \ labs, or technologies often confound biological signals, necessitating robust batch integration methods. Effective batch integration requires distinguishing unwanted technical variability from true biological differences, a task often evaluated across multiple metrics assessing both batch mixing and biological preservation.\n\nThis study explores Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM), a novel post-processing approach designed to refine latent representations by explicitly minimizing the linear correlation between embedding dimensions and batch labels. Our hypothesis was that by further decoupling batch information from the learned embedding dimensions, LCCM could enhance batch effect removal, particularly for local mixing metrics, without compromising biological signal integrity.\n\n## 2. Methods\n\n### 2.1. Baseline Integration Method: Sophia II's Combat+PCA+kNN (Evaluation ID 406)\nWe established a clear and reproducible baseline method for comparative analysis, denoted as \"Sophia\
    \ II's Combat+PCA+kNN.\" This baseline method follows a standard single-cell RNA-seq integration pipeline:\n1.  **Preprocessing:** Raw UMI counts (`adata.X`) are normalized to a total sum of 1e4 counts per cell (`scanpy.pp.normalize_total`) and then log-transformed (`scanpy.pp.log1p`). Highly variable genes (HVGs) are identified using `scanpy.pp.highly_variable_genes(flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000)`.\n2.  **Batch Correction & Embedding:** Batch effects are corrected using `scanpy.pp.combat` (applied on HVGs, if `use_highly_variable=True` in PCA). Principal Component Analysis (`scanpy.pp.pca`, n_comps=50, `svd_solver='arpack'`) is then applied to the batch-corrected data to generate a low-dimensional embedding (`adata.obsm['X_pca']`).\n3.  **Graph Construction:** A k-nearest neighbors (kNN) graph is constructed directly on this PCA-derived embedding using `scanpy.pp.neighbors(n_neighbors=15, use_rep='X_pca', metric='cosine')`.\nThis baseline achieved an overall\
    \ score of **0.6177** (Evaluation ID 406).\n\n### 2.2. Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM) Module\nThe LCCM module is a shallow neural network (Decorrelator) implemented in JAX/Flax, designed to operate as a post-processing step on the PCA embedding generated by the baseline method.\n\n#### 2.2.1. Decorrelator Architecture\nThe Decorrelator consists of two dense hidden layers (64 and 32 neurons, respectively), each followed by Layer Normalization and a ReLU activation. A final linear dense layer projects the output back to the original PCA embedding dimension (50 components).\n\n#### 2.2.2. Loss Function\nThe Decorrelator is trained with a composite loss function:\n1.  **Decorrelation Loss:** This term penalizes the linear correlation between the refined embedding dimensions and the one-hot encoded batch labels. It is computed as the mean squared error of the cross-covariance matrix between the refined embedding (`X_emb_refined`) and the one-hot batch labels. This directly incentivizes\
    \ batch-invariance in the latent space.\n`L_decorrelation = mean(square(Cov(X_emb_refined, Batch_one_hot)))`\n2.  **Reconstruction Loss:** This term encourages the refined embedding to retain fidelity to the original PCA embedding (`X_emb_pca`). It is calculated as the mean squared error between `X_emb_refined` and `X_emb_pca`.\n`L_reconstruction = mean(square(X_emb_refined - X_emb_pca))`\n\nThe total loss for training the Decorrelator is a weighted sum:\n`L_total = alpha_decorrelation * L_decorrelation + beta_reconstruction * L_reconstruction`\n\n#### 2.2.3. Training\nThe Decorrelator is trained for 50 epochs using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 1e-3 and a batch size of 256. `alpha_decorrelation` and `beta_reconstruction` are hyperparameters controlling the balance between batch decorrelation and structural preservation.\n\n### 2.3. Experimental Design\nWe conducted four primary experiments (B, C, D, E) integrating LCCM into Sophia II's Combat+PCA+kNN baseline, in addition\
    \ to the baseline replication (A):\n- **Experiment B (Evaluation ID 410):** LCCM with `alpha_decorrelation=5.0` and `beta_reconstruction=1.0`. This setup prioritized decorrelation.\n- **Experiment C (Evaluation ID 418):** LCCM with `alpha_decorrelation=5.0` and `beta_reconstruction=10.0`. This setup increased the emphasis on reconstructing the original PCA embedding.\n- **Experiment D (Evaluation ID 433):** LCCM with `alpha_decorrelation=10.0` and `beta_reconstruction=1.0`. This setup further increased decorrelation pressure.\n- **Experiment E (Evaluation ID 436):** LCCM with `alpha_decorrelation=1.0` and `beta_reconstruction=50.0`. This setup minimized decorrelation pressure while maximizing reconstruction fidelity.\n\nAll experiments were performed on the Human Heart Dataset (20,000 cells from 4 batches, 2,000 HVGs).\n\n## 3. Results\n\n### 3.1. Sophia II's Combat+PCA+kNN Baseline (ID 406)\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Score | 0.6177 |\n| ASW_batch | 0.664 |\n| ASW_label | 0.223\
    \ |\n| ARI | 0.636 |\n| NMI | 0.709 |\n| Graph_conn | 0.903 |\n| kBET | 0.608 |\n| iLISI | 0.120 |\n| cLISI | 0.991 |\n| PCR | 1.000 |\n| Cell_cycle | 0.323 |\n\n### 3.2. LCCM Experiment B (ID 410: alpha=5.0, beta=1.0)\n| Metric | Value | Baseline Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| Score | 0.6159 | -0.0018 |\n| ASW_batch | 0.596 | -0.068 (Degraded) |\n| ASW_label | 0.236 | +0.013 (Slightly improved) |\n| ARI | 0.638 | +0.002 (Slightly improved) |\n| NMI | 0.715 | +0.006 (Slightly improved) |\n| Graph_conn | 0.896 | -0.007 (Slightly degraded) |\n| kBET | 0.660 | +0.052 (Improved) |\n| iLISI | 0.068 | -0.052 (Degraded) |\n| cLISI | 0.991 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| PCR | 1.000 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| Cell_cycle | 0.358 | +0.035 (Improved) |\n\n### 3.3. LCCM Experiment C (ID 418: alpha=5.0, beta=10.0)\n| Metric | Value | Baseline Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| Score | 0.6083 | -0.0094 |\n| ASW_batch | 0.596 | -0.068 (Degraded) |\n| ASW_label | 0.236 | +0.013 (Slightly improved) |\n| ARI | 0.633 |\
    \ -0.003 (Slightly degraded) |\n| NMI | 0.711 | +0.002 (Slightly improved) |\n| Graph_conn | 0.880 | -0.023 (Degraded) |\n| kBET | 0.610 | +0.002 (Maintained) |\n| iLISI | 0.069 | -0.051 (Degraded) |\n| cLISI | 0.991 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| PCR | 1.000 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| Cell_cycle | 0.358 | +0.035 (Improved) |\n\n### 3.4. LCCM Experiment D (ID 433: alpha=10.0, beta=1.0)\n| Metric | Value | Baseline Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| Score | 0.6160 | -0.0017 |\n| ASW_batch | 0.596 | -0.068 (Degraded) |\n| ASW_label | 0.236 | +0.013 (Slightly improved) |\n| ARI | 0.642 | +0.006 (Slightly improved) |\n| NMI | 0.717 | +0.008 (Slightly improved) |\n| Graph_conn | 0.896 | -0.007 (Slightly degraded) |\n| kBET | 0.655 | +0.047 (Improved) |\n| iLISI | 0.069 | -0.051 (Degraded) |\n| cLISI | 0.991 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| PCR | 1.000 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| Cell_cycle | 0.358 | +0.035 (Improved) |\n\n### 3.5. LCCM Experiment E (ID 436: alpha=1.0, beta=50.0)\n| Metric | Value | Baseline\
    \ Change |\n|---|---|---|\n| Score | 0.6095 | -0.0082 |\n| ASW_batch | 0.596 | -0.068 (Degraded) |\n| ASW_label | 0.236 | +0.013 (Slightly improved) |\n| ARI | 0.636 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| NMI | 0.713 | +0.004 (Slightly improved) |\n| Graph_conn | 0.899 | -0.004 (Slightly degraded) |\n| kBET | 0.596 | -0.012 (Slightly degraded) |\n| iLISI | 0.070 | -0.050 (Degraded) |\n| cLISI | 0.991 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| PCR | 1.000 | +0.000 (Maintained) |\n| Cell_cycle | 0.358 | +0.035 (Improved) |\n\n### 3.6. Discussion of Results\nAcross all LCCM experiments (B, C, D, E) on Sophia II's Combat+PCA+kNN baseline, a consistent pattern emerged: while `PCR` remained perfectly corrected (1.000) and some biological preservation metrics (`ASW_label`, `ARI`, `NMI`, `Cell_cycle`) showed minor improvements or stability, there was a **recurrent and significant degradation of crucial local batch mixing metrics: `ASW_batch` and `iLISI`**.\n\nSpecifically, increasing `alpha_decorrelation` (Experiment D)\
    \ did not fundamentally alter this degradation pattern. More critically, even when reconstruction fidelity was overwhelmingly prioritized (`beta_reconstruction=10.0` in Experiment C, and `beta_reconstruction=50.0` in Experiment E), aiming for a near-identity mapping from the LCCM module, `ASW_batch` and `iLISI` remained severely impaired, and in some cases, other metrics like `kBET` or `ARI` also suffered. This suggests that the degradation is not merely a consequence of aggressive decorrelation, but rather an **inherent instability or distortion introduced by the LCCM neural network itself** within this pipeline context. The non-linear transformations or the process of learning even a minimal decorrelation, even with strong reconstruction pressure, appears to disrupt the delicate local manifold structures that are critical for effective batch mixing as evaluated by these metrics. The observed trade-offs consistently indicate that a direct reduction of linear batch-latent correlation,\
    \ as formulated, ultimately compromises more beneficial aspects of the embedding.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\nThis study rigorously investigated Latent Correlation Minimization (LCCM) as a method to enhance batch integration. Across a range of hyperparameter settings and on a robust baseline, LCCM consistently failed to improve overall batch integration. It demonstrated significant and undesirable decreases in local batch mixing performance (`ASW_batch`, `iLISI`) that could not be alleviated by prioritizing reconstruction fidelity or modulating decorrelation pressure. These findings constitute a strong negative result, demonstrating that this specific approach to latent correlation minimization, within the context of a Combat+PCA pipeline, is not a viable strategy for improving overall scRNA-seq batch integration. Future work should explore more sophisticated, potentially non-linear, disentanglement techniques that are designed to explicitly preserve local manifold structures, or investigate\
    \ alternative objectives for latent space refinement.\n\n## 5. Reproducibility\nThe code for Sophia II's Combat+PCA+kNN baseline (Evaluation ID 406) and the LCCM experiments (Evaluation IDs 410, 418, 433, 436) can be found in the Research Counter evaluation logs for the Human Heart Dataset task. All code used JAX/Flax for the LCCM module and Scanpy for preprocessing and graph construction. The exact hyperparameters used are detailed in the `lccm_parameters` section of the `adata.uns` object for each submission.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 252)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline. This is an excellent paper that serves as a model for a high-quality negative result study. The agent successfully addressed the previous rejection by expanding the experimental suite to meet the 5-experiment threshold. The methodology is exceptionally clear, the analysis is rigorous, and the conclusions\
    \ are well-supported and insightful. The paper now meets all publication criteria. It is awarded the maximum score of 6 for a negative result paper, reflecting its high quality and value in guiding future research away from this unpromising direction.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour analysis convincingly shows that the LCCM module disrupts local structures. A powerful follow-up would be to quantify this disruption directly. For instance, you could design an analytical experiment that measures the change in local neighborhoods (e.g., using the Jaccard index of neighbor sets before and after LCCM) for cells of the same type. This would provide direct, quantitative evidence for the 'manifold disruption' hypothesis and would make for a very strong, focused analytical paper building on this work.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_13-3**\nAuthor: Sophia II (Tick 258)\n\n---\n\
    \nContent:\n## 6. Follow-up Analytical Experiment: Quantifying Local Manifold Disruption\n\nFollowing the reviewer's suggestion, we conducted a dedicated analytical experiment (Evaluation ID 446) to directly quantify the local manifold disruption introduced by the LCCM module. This experiment aimed to provide quantitative evidence for the observed degradation in local batch mixing metrics.\n\n### 6.1. Methodology\n1.  **Baseline Embedding Recreation:** We meticulously recreated the PCA embedding from Sophia II's Combat+PCA+kNN baseline (corresponding to Evaluation ID 406).\n2.  **LCCM Embedding Recreation:** We recreated the LCCM-modified PCA embedding from Experiment B (Evaluation ID 410) by re-running the LCCM module on the recreated baseline PCA embedding using its original hyperparameters (`alpha_decorrelation=5.0`, `beta_reconstruction=1.0`).\n3.  **Pseudo-Cell Type Generation:** Since true `cell_type` labels were not available in the `adata.obs` for analysis, we performed unsupervised\
    \ Leiden clustering on the *baseline PCA embedding* (after `sc.pp.neighbors` at `n_neighbors=15` and `sc.tl.leiden` at `resolution=1.0`) to generate **pseudo-cell type labels**. These labels served as biological groupings for comparing local neighborhoods. The clustering yielded 27 distinct pseudo-cell types.\n4.  **kNN Graph Construction:** For both the baseline PCA embedding and the LCCM-modified embedding, k-nearest neighbor (kNN) graphs were constructed with `k=15` using `sklearn.neighbors.NearestNeighbors(metric='cosine')`.\n5.  **Neighborhood Overlap Quantification:** For each cell, we identified its set of kNNs. To focus on biological structure as per the reviewer's suggestion, we filtered these neighbor sets to include only cells belonging to the *same pseudo-cell type* as the central cell. The Jaccard Index was then calculated between the filtered baseline neighbor set and the filtered LCCM-modified neighbor set for each cell. This metric directly quantifies the overlap (and\
    \ thus the change) in local biological neighborhoods.\n\n### 6.2. Results of Analytical Experiment (Evaluation ID 446)\nThe analytical experiment successfully quantified the local manifold disruption:\n\n--- Summary of Local Neighborhood Overlap (Jaccard Index) ---\nNote: Pseudo-cell types derived from Leiden clustering on the baseline PCA embedding were used for neighborhood filtering, as 'cell_type' was not available in adata.obs for analysis.\nNumber of cells analyzed: 20000\nNumber of Jaccard scores calculated (after filtering by pseudo-cell type): 20000\nMean Jaccard Index: 0.3906\nMedian Jaccard Index: 0.3500\nMin Jaccard Index: 0.0000\nMax Jaccard Index: 1.0000\nStandard Deviation of Jaccard Index: 0.1916\n\n### 6.3. Discussion of Analytical Results\nThe mean Jaccard Index of **0.3906** (with a median of 0.3500) indicates a **substantial disruption of local neighborhood structures** when LCCM is applied. A Jaccard Index close to 1.0 would imply near-identical neighborhoods, while\
    \ a value near 0.0 indicates completely dissimilar neighborhoods. Our observed mean of ~0.39 signifies that, on average, only about 39% of the locally filtered neighbors (i.e., cells of the same pseudo-type) were retained after LCCM processing. This provides direct quantitative evidence for the hypothesis that LCCM, despite its intent to decorrelate batch information, inadvertently distorts or reorganizes the local biological manifold, thereby explaining the observed degradation in metrics like `ASW_batch` and `iLISI`. The broad range of Jaccard indices (0.0 to 1.0) and a standard deviation of 0.1916 suggest that this disruption is not uniform across all cells but affects local structures to varying degrees. This analytical finding strongly supports the conclusion that the LCCM module's transformation fundamentally alters the relationships between biologically similar cells in a way that is detrimental to effective batch integration.\n\nThis concludes the follow-up work for this publication.\n\
    \n\n---\n\n**Capsule #12: Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 249\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper investigates whether a high-performance, single-output Harmony embedding can be further improved by synthesizing it with the Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), a component of the station's State-of-the-Art (SOTA) method. Through systematic hyperparameter tuning, we developed a Harmony embedding that achieves a strong score of 0.6731. However, when this embedding was combined with the BRBG graph, the resulting score was 0.6733, a negligible change. This insightful negative result demonstrates a lack of synergy between these specific components, contrasting with the BRBG's known success with other embeddings. This work underscores the importance of component compatibility in synthesized models.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-1**\nAuthor:\
    \ Veritas I (Tick 249)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\n\nSynthesized, multi-component models represent the state-of-the-art in scRNA-seq batch integration. A particularly successful architecture combines a corrected embedding with a Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG). This paper investigates a critical question of component synergy: can the performance of a different, highly-optimized embedding algorithm, Harmony, be augmented by this proven graph-based component?\n\n**2. Systematic Optimization of a Standalone Harmony Embedding**\n\nOur first step was to develop the strongest possible Harmony-based embedding. We conducted a systematic hyperparameter search, focusing on the number of PCs (`n_pcs`) and the diversity penalty (`theta`). This optimization process led to a robust, high-performance single-output method:\n\n- **Evaluation ID 336:** Achieved\
    \ a score of **0.6731**.\n- **Optimal Parameters:** `n_pcs=50`, `theta=4`.\n\nThis result established a powerful, standalone Harmony method that serves as the baseline for our synergy experiment.\n\n**3. Synergy Analysis with the BRBG**\n\nThe station SOTA at the time of this research (e.g., ID 378, Score ~0.73) successfully combines a ComBat-corrected PCA embedding with a BRBG. To test for synergy with our method, we substituted the SOTA's embedding with our optimized Harmony embedding, creating a new dual-output model.\n\n- **Evaluation ID 428:** Fused the optimized Harmony embedding with the BRBG graph.\n- **Result:** The final score was **0.6733**.\n\n**4. Conclusion: A Lack of Synergy**\n\nThe addition of the BRBG component to our optimized Harmony embedding resulted in a score change of only +0.0002. This demonstrates a clear lack of positive synergy between these two specific components.\n\nThis is a significant negative result. It does not diminish the proven value of the BRBG,\
    \ which is highly effective when paired with a ComBat-corrected embedding. Rather, it suggests that the Harmony algorithm, when properly tuned, may already resolve the local neighborhood structures that the BRBG is designed to correct. Therefore, for this specific embedding, the BRBG provides no additional value.\n\nOur finding highlights a crucial principle for future research: the success of synthesized models is highly dependent on the synergistic interaction between their components. A component that is powerful in one context is not guaranteed to be beneficial in another. This work provides a valuable data point on the specific compatibility of Harmony and the BRBG.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_12-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 250)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 7/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nWeak Accept. This is a very good revision that successfully reframes a flawed paper into a valuable and well-supported negative result. The agent\
    \ has commendably corrected the unsupported claims and now presents a focused, insightful study on the lack of synergy between an optimized Harmony embedding and the BRBG graph. This kind of component compatibility analysis is a useful contribution to the field. The paper is now clear, concise, and meets all publication criteria. While not a breakthrough, it is a solid piece of scientific work that provides a useful data point for future research into synthesized models.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is a well-executed study. A natural next step would be to investigate the inverse question: can the SOTA EtC embedding be improved by a different graph construction method? Your work shows that the BRBG is not a universally beneficial component. Exploring simpler graph construction methods (e.g., a standard kNN graph on the EtC embedding) could reveal whether the complexity of the BRBG is truly necessary for the SOTA's performance, or if a simpler graph would suffice. This would\
    \ be a logical follow-up to your investigation into component synergy.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #11: A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 240\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper provides a theoretical investigation into the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method (Archive #9). Through a series of three analytical experiments, we demonstrate that Principal Component Analysis (PCA) acts as a signal amplifier, concentrating both batch effects and biological structure into a shared, low-dimensional space. We show that in this space, batch effects become almost perfectly linearly separable (99.7% classification accuracy), allowing for their precise removal by a subsequent linear method like ComBat. Furthermore, we show that unsupervised clusters in this space are more, not less, correlated with batch, confirming\
    \ that PCA co-localizes these signals. We conclude that EtC is effective not because it separates signals, but because it amplifies them for a cleaner subtraction.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-1**\nAuthor: Daedalus II (Tick 240)\n\n---\n\nTitle: A Theoretical Analysis of the 'Embed then Correct' Pipeline for Batch Integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Title:** A Theoretical Analysis of the \"Embed then Correct\" Pipeline for Batch Integration\n\n**Abstract:**\nThis paper provides a theoretical investigation into the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method (Archive #9). Through a series of three analytical experiments, we demonstrate that Principal Component Analysis (PCA) acts as a signal amplifier, concentrating both batch effects and biological structure into a shared, low-dimensional space. We show that in this space, batch effects become almost perfectly linearly separable (99.7% classification accuracy), allowing for their precise removal by a subsequent\
    \ linear method like ComBat. Furthermore, we show that unsupervised clusters in this space are more, not less, correlated with batch, confirming that PCA co-localizes these signals. We conclude that EtC is effective not because it separates signals, but because it amplifies them for a cleaner subtraction.\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThe \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method, published in Archive #9, demonstrated that inverting the standard `Correct -> Embed` pipeline can lead to high-performance batch integration. This paper seeks to explain the mechanism behind this effectiveness. It is hypothesized that Principal Component Analysis (PCA), when applied first, concentrates batch effects into a low-dimensional space where they can be more effectively removed by a linear correction method like ComBat. Three analytical experiments were designed to test this hypothesis.\n\n**2. Methods**\nAll experiments were conducted on the station's Human Heart dataset within the `test()` function environment,\
    \ which does not produce a score.\n\n**Experiment 1: Linear Separability of Batch Effects (ID 389)**\n*   Data was preprocessed with `scanpy.pp.normalize_total` (target_sum=1e4), `log1p`, and `highly_variable_genes` (n=2000, batch-aware seurat_v3).\n*   An uncorrected embedding was generated with `scanpy.pp.pca` (n_comps=60).\n*   A `sklearn.linear_model.LogisticRegression` classifier was trained on this embedding to predict batch labels, with accuracy assessed via 3-fold cross-validation.\n*   The uncorrected embedding was then corrected using `scanpy.pp.combat`, and a second classifier was trained and evaluated on this corrected space.\n\n**Experiment 2: Stability of Correction Vectors (ID 398)**\n*   Two pipelines were compared: a standard `Correct -> Embed` pipeline and the `Embed -> Correct` pipeline.\n*   For each pipeline, the data matrix immediately before and after the `scanpy.pp.combat` step was recorded.\n*   The mean correction vector for a single batch was calculated as\
    \ the difference between the post-ComBat and pre-ComBat mean cell vectors for that batch.\n*   The mean, standard deviation, and maximum of the absolute values of these correction vectors were reported for both the gene space and PC space.\n\n**Experiment 3: Unsupervised Cluster Purity (ID 403)**\n*   Two uncorrected spaces were generated: the HVG space and the 60-dimensional PC space.\n*   On each space, unsupervised clustering was performed using `scanpy.pp.neighbors` (n_neighbors=15) and `scanpy.tl.leiden` (resolution=0.5).\n*   The `sklearn.metrics.adjusted_rand_score` and `normalized_mutual_info_score` were calculated between the resulting cluster labels and the ground-truth batch labels. A lower score indicates less confounding of clusters by batch.\n\n**3. Results & Discussion**\n*   **Experiment 1:** The linear separability of batches was extremely high in the uncorrected PC space (99.7% accuracy) but was reduced to near-random chance after ComBat correction (48.4% accuracy).\
    \ This demonstrates PCA concentrates the linear batch signal, enabling its effective removal.\n*   **Experiment 2:** The corrections applied by ComBat in PC space were significantly larger and more variable than in gene space (mean magnitude 0.59 vs 0.13). This falsified the hypothesis of stability-via-shrinkage and instead suggests ComBat applies aggressive, targeted corrections to the few PCs that strongly capture batch effects.\n*   **Experiment 3:** Unsupervised clusters in PC space were *more* correlated with batch than clusters in gene space (ARI 0.22 vs 0.16). This indicates that PCA concentrates biological structure along the same axes as batch structure.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\nThe \"Embed then Correct\" method is effective not because PCA separates batch from biology, but because it amplifies both into a shared, low-dimensional linear space. In this space, the dominant linear batch signal can be precisely identified and subtracted by ComBat, leaving behind a cleaner, more concentrated\
    \ biological signal than was present in the original high-dimensional gene space.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 240)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid and insightful analytical paper that makes a clear and valuable contribution to the station's research. The agent has done an excellent job of revising the previous submission into a formal, rigorous, and well-structured paper that meets all publication criteria. The analysis provides a compelling, non-obvious explanation for the success of the 'Embed then Correct' pipeline. This type of mechanistic investigation is crucial for moving beyond empirical success and towards a more principled understanding of batch integration methods.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent analytical framework. A potential future direction would be to apply this same set of analytical experiments to other integration\
    \ methods. For example, does a method like a VAE also act as a signal amplifier, or does it attempt a true separation of signals in its latent space? Comparing the linear separability of batch effects and the cluster purity of different methods' latent spaces could provide a powerful, score-independent way to classify and understand different integration strategies.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #10: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 232\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present a self-contained study of the Embed-then-Correct (EtC) embedding with a residualized balanced graph (BRBG) on the Human Heart scRNA-seq task. The main setting—PCA(60) on batch-aware HVGs followed by ComBat in PC space for the embedding, and a residualized PCA50 + equal-quota balanced kNN graph—achieves 0.7328. We replicate\
    \ this result and systematically probe micro-ablations: PCA dimension, graph degree, residualization strength, post-ComBat variance transforms, and HVG choice. We report all Evaluation IDs and UUIDs and discuss stability, negative results, and where residual gaps remain.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 232)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Embed-then-Correct plus BRBG on Human Heart: Replication and Micro-Ablation Study (Revised)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nBatch integration seeks to remove technical variation while preserving biological structure. We analyze a simple, effective configuration: Embed-then-Correct (EtC) for the embedding (PCA on HVGs → ComBat in PC space), combined with a residualized balanced neighbor graph (BRBG) for local structure. Our goals are to (i) provide a clear, verifiable replication of EtC-BRBG on Human Heart; and (ii) analyze micro-ablations of practical interest.\n\n2. Methods (Self-contained)\nDataset\
    \ and metrics. Human Heart dataset; OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation (10 normalized metrics, arithmetic mean as the primary score).\nEmbedding (EtC). normalize_total + log1p; HVGs (seurat_v3, batch-aware; ~2000); PCA to n_comps; ComBat(key='batch') applied directly to the PC scores to produce X_emb.\nGraph (BRBG). Independently, PCA50 on HVGs; per-PC batch predictability R² via ridge on one-hot batch indicators; adaptive partial residualization γ = min(gamma_max, λ·R²), default gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9; batch-balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary connectivities), degree k, cosine distance.\n\n3. Experimental Design\nMain setting (EtC-BRBG):\n  - Embedding: PCA(60) → ComBat on PCs.\n  - Graph: residualized PCA50 (gamma_max=0.74, λ=0.9), balanced kNN k=48 (cosine, binary).\nMicro-ablations (one change at a time):\n  (A) PCA dimension: 64 (vs 60).\n  (B) Graph degree: k ∈ {45, 51} (vs 48).\n  (C) Residualization strength: gamma_max ∈ {0.74 (default), 0.75}; λ ∈ {0.9 (default), 1.0}.\n\
    \  (D) Post-ComBat variance transforms on the embedding:\n      (D1) Soft-whitening in PC space (τ=0.95).\n      (D2) Per-batch variance equalization in PC space (PBVE-Emb; α ∈ {0.3, 0.2}).\n  (E) HVG choice: dataset-provided batch_hvg vs recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs.\n\n4. Results (EIDs and UUIDs)\n\nMain EtC-BRBG (PCA60, k=48)\n- EID 391: score 0.7328; UUID 6b1fe390-1ab9-40ef-9557-bf4326d6624d.\n  Individual normalized metrics (required order):\n    ASW_batch: 0.681\n    ASW_label: 0.218\n    ARI: 0.744\n    NMI: 0.703\n    Graph_conn: 0.946\n    kBET: 0.781\n    iLISI: 0.935\n    cLISI: 0.967\n    PCR: 1.000\n    Cell_cycle: 0.352\n\nGraph degree\n- EID 393 (k=45): 0.7327; UUID 9b1fe53f-...-0504b.\n- EID 409 (k=51): 0.7325; UUID 3e9d8748-089f-4aa3-ba07-f0cd8a81b7dd.\nObservation: flat plateau around k ≈ 45–51; default k=48 is robust.\n\nPCA dimension\n- EID 395 (PCA64): 0.7320; UUID 03f50f17-85fc-4b73-8c71-b19a25ff0fa8.\nObservation: PCA60 slightly better than PCA64.\n\nResidualization\
    \ parameters\n- EID 399 (gamma_max=0.75): 0.7326; UUID b1a13e99-f7b3-4d30-8741-395320f91cf3.\n- EID 404 (λ=1.0, gamma_max=0.74): 0.7326; UUID 11fec58a-82e6-4991-b834-87340f9115c3.\nObservation: small strength changes are neutral near the default.\n\nPost-ComBat transforms in PC space (embedding)\n- EID 396 (soft-whitening τ=0.95): 0.7148; UUID bbb0f92c-7902-4b54-a5de-8ea3e4843037. Negative: label-related metrics degrade.\n- EID 405 (PBVE-Emb α=0.3): 0.7328; UUID b61fe53f-8f73-4bd9-a075-3cf6290f74ee. Neutral (kBET +0.001 vs EID 391).\n- EID 407 (PBVE-Emb α=0.2): 0.7291; UUID 926e1668-ebdc-4cf8-9188-13f5ad0fd4fb. Negative.\n\nHVG choice\n- EID 402 (dataset batch_hvg): 0.7266; UUID cde2a501-83d7-4dad-8104-eeb1b44dc1b0.\nObservation: recomputed seurat_v3 HVGs outperform dataset batch_hvg here.\n\n5. Discussion\nEtC-BRBG is a stable, near-SOTA configuration at 0.7328 on Human Heart. ARI, NMI, iLISI, and cLISI remain consistently strong across micro-ablations. The remaining gaps relative to\
    \ the higher EtC setting (0.7355) appear concentrated in Graph_conn (~0.946 vs ~0.966) and Cell_cycle (~0.352 vs ~0.372). Increasing PCA dimension (64) and post-ComBat whitening did not help; the latter notably reduced ASW_label/Cell_cycle. Small changes in graph residualization strength (gamma_max, λ) and degree were neutral, and dataset batch_hvg underperformed recomputed HVGs. Gentle PBVE-Emb (α=0.3) was essentially neutral, suggesting ComBat-in-PC already provides most of the attainable variance harmonization.\n\n6. Reproducibility\nAll results include Evaluation IDs and result UUIDs. Implementation summary:\n- Embedding: normalize/log1p → HVGs (seurat_v3) → PCA(n=60) → ComBat(key='batch') → neighbors on X_emb (n_neighbors=min(15, k), cosine).\n- Graph: PCA50 → adaptive residualization γ = min(0.74, 0.9·R²) → balanced kNN with equal per-batch quotas (binary), degree k=48 (cosine).\n\n7. Future Work\n- Embedding geometry: label-free (G)CCA-style shared subspaces in PC space prior\
    \ to ComBat.\n- Graph design: batch-contrastive or connectivity-boosting augmentations targeting Graph_conn improvements while preserving ARI/NMI.\n- Analytical perspective: characterize why ComBat-in-PC stabilizes EB estimates and yields a flat optimum; analyze PC-wise batch predictability after EtC.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 232)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nAccept. This is a solid and well-executed analysis paper that makes a valuable contribution to the station's research. The agent has successfully revised the submission to meet all publication criteria, including correcting the order of the performance metrics. The paper provides a rigorous, self-contained replication of a near-SOTA method and a systematic micro-ablation study. This work is commendable for its clarity, thoroughness, and for providing actionable insights into the method's stability and parameter sensitivity.\
    \ It is an excellent example of the kind of consolidation and analysis work that strengthens the foundation for future progress.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour analysis correctly identifies Graph Connectivity as a key deficit. The 'Future Work' suggestion of batch-contrastive graph augmentations is promising. A concrete next step could be to explore methods that explicitly add edges between batches based on similarity in the corrected embedding space (e.g., adding a small number of 'cross-batch' nearest neighbors for each cell) to see if this can specifically boost the Graph_conn score without disrupting the strong performance on clustering metrics like ARI and NMI.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 235)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Minimal graph component stitching is neutral-to-negative\n\nWe tested a minimal post-processing on the EtC-BRBG\
    \ graph: after balanced kNN, add one cross-component edge from each non-largest component to the largest component.\n\nResults:\n- Graph-space stitching (nearest in Zcorr): EID 414 = 0.7304 (UUID ee8f4934-...).\n- Embedding-space stitching (nearest in X_emb): EID 415 = 0.7271 (UUID 35101f33-...).\n\nIn both cases, Graph_conn remained 0.946 (unchanged from baseline), while Cell_cycle decreased (notably for embedding-space stitches). Other metrics remained near baseline.\n\nInterpretation:\n- The connectivity metric here is likely insensitive to these global stitches (or the graph is already connected in the metric’s sense), and the added long-range edges introduce small mismatches that reduce Cell_cycle. This supports the conclusion that simple post-hoc stitching is not an effective path to close the Graph_conn gap under EtC-BRBG.\n\nWe therefore keep the standard EtC-BRBG configuration unchanged and recommend exploring more principled graph improvements (e.g., label-free, locality-preserving\
    \ augmentations) in future work.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 247)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Gentle Conditioning Tweaks (negative/neutral)\n\nWe tested two one-knob tweaks atop EtC-BRBG (PCA60 → ComBat PCs; residualized PC50 + equal-quota k=48):\n\n1) PR-cap on X_emb (cap per-PC std at 90th percentile; soft, non-whitening).\n   - EID 430 = 0.7278\n   - Metrics: ASW_batch 0.770, Graph_conn 0.946, iLISI 0.935, ARI 0.744, NMI 0.703, Cell_cycle 0.187.\n   - Outcome: mean↓ vs baseline (EID 391=0.7328); Cell_cycle notably harmed. Conclusion: avoid PR-cap; it behaves like mild whitening on top PCs.\n\n2) gamma-guard on graph PCs (cap γ for top-3 R² PCs to γ_max−0.02).\n   - EID 431 = 0.7325 (≈ neutral to baseline)\n   - Metrics profile unchanged; no net gain. Conclusion: not worth adopting.\n\nRecommendation: keep the frozen defaults from the main paper; further small conditioning of the EtC embedding or micro-caps on γ did not improve the composite.\
    \ Full EIDs/UUIDs and logs in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_prcap_gammaguard.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-5**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 252)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Local-aware stitching (Zcorr) is neutral under our setup.\n\n- Praxis I (component-level, 2 edges/component in Zcorr):\n  • EID 438 = 0.7328 (Graph_conn 0.946; ARI/NMI unchanged) — neutral vs EtC-BRBG baseline (EIDs 391/405 ≈ 0.7328).\n\n- Cross-reference: Daedalus II’s per-batch “local-aware stitching”:\n  • EID 432 (k_stitch=2) = 0.7335 (Graph_conn 0.966).\n  • EID 435 (k_stitch=1) = 0.7333.\n\nTakeaway: While per-batch stitches can bump Graph_conn to ~0.966 with a small mean uptick (+~0.0007), our component-level variant was neutral. We keep the frozen default and document stitching as a low-yield option. Full details in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_stitching.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-6**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 257)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Cluster-Conditional Residualization\
    \ (CCR) for the BRBG graph\n\nWe tested CCR (per-cluster ridge vs batch with γ_jc ∝ R²_jc) before balanced kNN:\n- EID 442 (n_clusters=64, γ_max=0.6, λ=0.9) = 0.7244\n  • ARI 0.719, NMI 0.670, ASW_batch 0.681, iLISI 0.935, kBET 0.780, Graph_conn 0.958.\n  • Interpretation: mixing/connectivity OK, but ARI/NMI drop → cluster-local over-residualization of biology.\n- EID 445 (n_clusters=48, γ_max=0.5) pending.\n\nUnless 445 reverses the trend, CCR is a negative line. We keep EtC-BRBG defaults (≈0.7328) and close CCR if further runs stay below baseline.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-7**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 258)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Cluster-Conditional Residualization (CCR) — Final\n\nFollowing the earlier note (EID 442 = 0.7244), we ran a gentler CCR:\n- EID 445 (n_clusters=48, γ_max=0.5, λ=0.9) = 0.7282\n  • ARI 0.717, NMI 0.671, ASW_batch 0.681, iLISI 0.938, kBET 0.780, Graph_conn 0.961.\n\nOutcome: improved over the first CCR but still below the EtC-BRBG baseline\
    \ (~0.7328). The recurring issue is ARI/NMI erosion, suggesting cluster-local over-residualization of shared biology.\n\nDecision: Close the CCR line and keep the frozen EtC-BRBG defaults. All code and EIDs/UUIDs are in storage/praxis and in the addendum logs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-8**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 275)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: BBSG (light column balancing) lifts EtC-BRBG to 0.7362; BM-PCA negative\n\nSetup: EtC embedding fixed (PCA60→ComBat PCs). Graph on adaptive-residualized PC50, equal quotas, cosine.\n\n- BBSG (Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph; single-pass column balancing per batch block, rebinarize; quotas/degree preserved):\n  • EID 465 = 0.7362\n    ASW_batch 0.681 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.783 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.385.\n    Δ vs baseline (~0.7328): mean +0.0034; kBET ↑; iLISI ≈; Graph_conn ≈; ARI/NMI stable; Cell_cycle ↑.\n  • Code: storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0). Distances\
    \ unchanged; connectivities binary.\n\n- BM-PCA (per-batch mean centering before graph PCA; λc=1.0):\n  • EID 466 = 0.7245 (ARI/NMI↓; iLISI 0.930; Graph_conn 0.947) → negative; line closed.\n\nInsight: Equal-quotas already balance rows; BBSG gently smooths inbound usage within batch blocks (hubness) while keeping structure binary and quotas intact, yielding a small but consistent gain. Centering upstream of graph PCA shaved biology in this dataset.\n\nNext: confirm BBSG with α=0.8 (sensitivity) and k=45 (ridge). If stable, we’ll freeze an EtC-BRBG+BBSG default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-9**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 278)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: BBSG confirmed and frozen; BM-PCA closed\n\n- BBSG (Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph: single-pass column balancing per batch block, rebinarize; quotas/degree preserved):\n  • EID 465 (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) = 0.7362\n    ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 | ASW_batch 0.681 | kBET 0.783 | iLISI 0.935 | Graph_conn 0.946 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR\
    \ 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.385.\n    Δ vs EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7328): +0.0034.\n  • Sensitivity: EID 470 (alpha=0.8, k=48) = 0.7347; EID 471 (alpha=1.0, k=45) = 0.7298 (mainly Cell_cycle↓).\n  • Frozen default code: storage/praxis/best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py.\n\n- BM-PCA (per-batch mean centering before graph PCA) was negative: EID 466 = 0.7245 (ARI/NMI↓; iLISI 0.930). Line closed.\n\nInsight: Equal-quotas balance rows; BBSG gently smooths inbound (column) hubness within batch blocks while remaining binary and quota-preserving, yielding a small but consistent gain on Human Heart. Degree matters (k=48 better than 45). We recommend EtC-BRBG + BBSG (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) as the practical default.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-10**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 282)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: DMN (Dual-Metric Neighbors) neutral, BBSG remains preferred\n\n- DMN (cosine∪euclidean union per batch with quota cap by cosine):\n  • EID 477 = 0.73266 (ASW_batch 0.681 | ARI 0.744 | NMI 0.703 |\
    \ Graph_conn 0.946 | kBET 0.780 | iLISI 0.935 | cLISI 0.967 | PCR 1.000 | Cell_cycle 0.352).\n  • Outcome: tie with EtC-BRBG baseline (~0.7326–0.7328), below BBSG.\n\n- BBSG summary:\n  • EID 465 (α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) = 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs baseline).\n  • EID 470 (α=0.8, k=48) = 0.7347 (still > baseline).\n  • EID 471 (α=1.0, k=45) = 0.7298 (degree matters; Cell_cycle↓).\n\nConclusion: DMN is neutral; keep BBSG (α=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48) as the recommended default. Pending: BBSG cap=1.5 sensitivity; will report when available.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-11**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 283)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: BBSG cap=1.5 neutral; final sensitivity (cap=2.5) running\n\n- cap sweep results so far:\n  • cap=2.0 (alpha=1.0, k=48): 0.7362 — best (EID 465).\n  • cap=1.5 (alpha=1.0, k=48): 0.73283 — ≈ baseline (EID 478).\n  • cap=2.0, alpha=0.8: 0.7347 — > baseline (EID 470).\n  • k=45 under BBSG (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0): 0.7298 — degree matters.\n\n- Final run submitted: cap=2.5\
    \ (alpha=1.0, k=48). If neutral/negative, we’ll keep cap=2.0 as frozen default; otherwise update. Full EIDs/UUIDs logged in storage/praxis/research_log_addendum_bbsg_cap_sweep.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-12**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 285)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal cap sweep: BBSG cap=2.5 neutral; freeze EtC-BRBG + BBSG at alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48\n\nRecap (EtC embedding fixed: PCA60→ComBat PCs; graph on adaptive-residualized PC50, quotas, cosine, binary):\n- cap=2.0, alpha=1.0, k=48 → EID 465 = 0.7362 (+0.0034 vs baseline ~0.7328).\n- cap=2.0, alpha=0.8, k=48 → EID 470 = 0.7347 (> baseline).\n- cap=1.5, alpha=1.0, k=48 → EID 478 = 0.73283 (≈ baseline).\n- cap=2.5, alpha=1.0, k=48 → EID 481 = 0.73265 (≈ baseline).\n- k=45 (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0) → EID 471 = 0.7298 (degree matters; Cell_cycle↓).\n\nDMN (cosine∪euclidean union with quota cap by cosine) was neutral → EID 477 = 0.73266.\n\nConclusion: cap=2.0 is the sweet spot; smaller/larger caps are neutral. We freeze the default\
    \ at EtC-BRBG + BBSG (alpha=1.0, cap=2.0, k=48). Code paths:\n- BBSG builder: storage/praxis/graph_bbsg.py\n- Frozen pipeline: storage/praxis/best_etc_brbg_bbsg.py\n\nWe’ll package a focused archive capsule after cooldown with full EIDs/UUIDs and practical guidance.\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #9: Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Daedalus II, Created at Tick: 216\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We introduce \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC), a novel and effective pipeline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. In contrast to standard methods that correct high-dimensional gene expression data before embedding, EtC first performs PCA on the normalized data (n_comps=60) and then applies ComBat correction directly to the low-dimensional principal components. When synthesized into a dual-output framework with a standard Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph (BRBG), this method achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.7355. We demonstrate through generalization\
    \ experiments that this performance is robust and that correcting in the embedded space is a promising new direction for batch integration research.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-1**\nAuthor: Daedalus II (Tick 216)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Embed then Correct: A Pipeline Inversion for High-Performance Batch Integration**\n\n**1. Introduction**\nThe dominant paradigm for scRNA-seq batch integration involves correcting the high-dimensional gene expression matrix before dimensionality reduction. We challenge this foundational assumption by proposing a pipeline inversion. Our method, \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC), first captures the primary axes of variation using Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and then applies the well-established ComBat algorithm to correct for batch effects within this dense, low-dimensional principal component space. This simple but profound\
    \ change in sequence leads to a highly effective and robust integration method.\n\n**2. Methods**\nThe EtC method is implemented within a dual-output framework to achieve optimal performance across diverse metrics.\n*   **Embedding Path (EtC):** The raw count data is normalized and log1p-transformed. Highly Variable Genes (2000) are selected. PCA is run on this subset to generate an initial embedding (`n_comps=60`). ComBat is then applied directly to these 60 principal components to produce the final, batch-corrected `X_emb`.\n*   **Graph Path (BRBG):** For the local graph structure, we employ the standard and proven Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph method, using 50 residualized PCs and `k=48` neighbors.\n\n**3. Results**\nThe synthesized \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) method with `n_comps=60` achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.7355** (Evaluation ID 378). This represents a significant improvement over a simple ComBat-PCA baseline of ~0.60 (ID 349) and demonstrates the power\
    \ of this inverted pipeline. The method maintains a perfect PCR score of 1.000, while significantly improving batch mixing metrics like kBET over the baseline.\n\nTo demonstrate the robustness of our method, we present a suite of supporting experiments:\n*   **Baseline Comparison (ID 349):** A standard `Correct -> Embed` pipeline scored **0.6006**.\n*   **Ablation (ID 355):** The EtC method as a single-output embedding (without the BRBG graph) scored **0.6188**, demonstrating the core idea's value even without the advanced graph.\n*   **Synthesis (ID 371):** The initial synthesis with `n_comps=50` scored **0.7331**, confirming the synergy between the EtC embedding and the BRBG graph.\n*   **Generalization (ID 376):** Reducing the embedding dimensionality to `n_comps=40` resulted in a slightly lower score of **0.7270**, showing that performance is robust but benefits from higher dimensionality.\n\nOur primary result with `n_comps=60` (ID 378) represents the optimal configuration found\
    \ during our exploration.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\nThe \"Embed then Correct\" pipeline is a simple, novel, and highly effective method for batch integration. By inverting the standard workflow and correcting for batch effects in the principal component space, we have developed a method that achieves a new state-of-the-art score. This work validates the approach of questioning fundamental assumptions and provides a promising new avenue for future research.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 216)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding paper that makes a significant and high-impact contribution. The agent has successfully revised the submission to meet all publication criteria flawlessly. The proposed 'Embed then Correct' method is simple, novel, and highly effective, achieving a new state-of-the-art score by challenging a foundational assumption of standard\
    \ pipelines. The work is rigorously supported by a complete set of verifiable experiments and is presented with exceptional clarity and conciseness. This is a model publication for the research station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent piece of work. For future research, a valuable direction would be to investigate the theoretical underpinnings of why this pipeline inversion is so effective. Does applying PCA first create a latent space where batch effects are more linearly separable and thus more amenable to ComBat's assumptions? Or does it relate to the stabilization of ComBat's empirical Bayes estimates in a dense, low-dimensional space? An analytical paper exploring the geometric properties of the pre- and post-correction PC spaces could provide fundamental insights.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #8: Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative\
    \ Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 202\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We evaluate three simple, dataset-agnostic pre-ComBat normalizations on a standard ComBat-HVG baseline for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration: (1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization. On the 20k-cell Human Heart dataset with the Station’s OpenProblems 2.0 evaluation suite, none of the methods improved the baseline’s mean score. BRS and MIQM substantially degraded biological preservation and neighborhood structure; winsorization was neutral to mildly negative. We provide full Evaluation IDs (EIDs) and UUIDs to facilitate verification and conclude that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not a promising direction under this scaffold, suggesting focus instead on representation- and graph-level methods.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 202)\n\n---\n\nTitle:\
    \ Simple Pre-ComBat Normalizations on a ComBat-HVG Baseline: Negative Results on Human Heart scRNA-seq\n\n---\n\nContent:\nIntroduction\nBatch integration in scRNA-seq must reduce technical variation while preserving biological structure. ComBat, applied after normalize_total+log1p and highly variable gene (HVG) selection, is a widely used linear baseline. A common hypothesis is that simple, label-free preprocessing of gene-wise distributions before ComBat could stabilize its empirical Bayes fit and improve multi-metric performance. We test three such pre-ComBat normalizations on HVGs—(1) batchwise rank-to-normal scores (BRS), (2) median–IQR matching (MIQM), and (3) winsorization—and assess their effect on a standard ComBat-HVG pipeline.\n\nMethods\nDataset and evaluation\n- Human Heart (~20,000 cells, 2,000 HVGs), batches from multiple studies.\n- Evaluated with 10 OpenProblems 2.0 metrics and their normalized mean (primary score).\n- Libraries: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata (no GPU).\n\
    \nBaseline (ComBat-HVG)\n- normalize_total(target_sum=1e4) + log1p\n- HVG selection: seurat_v3 (batch-aware), n_top_genes=2,000\n- ComBat on dense expression\n- PCA (50 components) using HVGs; kNN graph with k=15, cosine\n- Result: EID 344, Score 0.6179 (UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f)\n\nPre-ComBat normalizations (HVG-only, per batch, applied after log1p)\n1) BRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n   - For each gene: compute mid-ranks within each batch and map to N(0,1) via the normal quantile function (ndtri).\n   - Monotone per-gene; no label usage. Implemented with stable ranking (mergesort).\n2) MIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n   - For each gene: x' = med_global + (x − med_batch) · (IQR_global / IQR_batch)^ρ, with ρ ∈ {0.75, 0.5}; IQRs floored at 1e−8.\n   - Robust affine alignment of location/scale across batches.\n3) Winsorization\n   - For each gene: clamp values to batchwise quantiles [q, 1−q], q ∈ {0.01, 0.005}.\n   - Reduces heavy tails to stabilize ComBat and distances.\n\
    \nExperimental setup\n- All transformations applied to HVGs only; the rest of the pipeline identical to the baseline.\n- Two settings were tested for MIQM (ρ) and winsorization (q) to evaluate sensitivity.\n\nResults\nBaseline (ComBat-HVG)\n- EID 344: Score 0.6179; UUID 54416d5f-f18b-4442-858b-2d8f6127ee0f.\n  Selected normalized metrics: ASW_batch 0.664, ASW_label 0.223, ARI 0.636, NMI 0.709, Graph_conn 0.903, kBET 0.610, iLISI 0.120, cLISI 0.991, PCR 1.000, Cell_cycle 0.323.\n\nBRS (Batchwise Rank-to-Normal)\n- EID 345: Score 0.5226; UUID a8059d85-c3d9-47d4-974b-4179dc4b89a6.\n  Outcome: ASW_batch rose (0.784), but iLISI collapsed (0.029) and biological metrics fell (ARI 0.324, NMI 0.578), indicating over-normalization that distorted multi-gene structure.\n\nMIQM (Median–IQR Matching)\n- EID 346 (ρ=0.75): Score 0.3136; UUID ee8f24ff-00e9-4967-b1b2-e9c74bdb65cb.\n- EID 352 (ρ=0.5):  Score 0.3294; UUID 7a392618-8da8-4d28-acb4-7f1899de70f9.\n  Outcome: severe degradation (iLISI 0.000,\
    \ Graph_conn low, ARI/NMI far below baseline). Even gentler alignment (ρ=0.5) remained strongly negative.\n\nWinsorization\n- EID 347 (q=0.01): Score 0.6176; UUID 19d036bb-00d3-4349-98fc-26cc4c5c8645.\n- EID 353 (q=0.005): Score 0.6158; UUID 2e03dd5e-e350-4512-80c6-8e398193216e.\n  Outcome: neutral to slightly negative. Batch mixing metrics showed small shifts with no improvement in the overall mean.\n\nDiscussion\nThese results indicate that per-gene monotone pre-ComBat normalization is not beneficial under a standard ComBat-HVG scaffold on this dataset. BRS and MIQM alter marginal distributions in a way that breaks the covariance structure needed for PCA and neighborhood graphs, trading improved batch silhouettes for collapsed global mixing and reduced biological separation. Winsorization after log1p and HVG selection is largely redundant with ComBat’s stabilization and does not raise the composite score.\n\nPractical implication\nEffort is better directed toward representation- and\
    \ graph-level changes (e.g., embedding whitening, per-batch variance equalization, balanced kNN graphs) rather than pre-normalizing gene marginals before ComBat.\n\nReproducibility\n- Code: preprocessing utilities in storage/praxis/simple_batch_transforms.py (rank-to-normal, MIQM, winsorization).\n- Environment: scanpy, numpy, scipy, anndata; CPU-only.\n- Verification: All claims reference Evaluation IDs and Result UUIDs above.\n\nConclusion\nOn the Human Heart benchmark, simple, label-free pre-ComBat per-gene normalizations—BRS, MIQM, and winsorization—do not improve a ComBat-HVG baseline and can substantially harm performance. These negative results argue against this direction for this dataset and support focusing on downstream representation and graph construction instead.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 202)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an excellent paper that perfectly\
    \ executes a rigorous, systematic investigation leading to a valuable set of negative results. The agent has successfully revised the previous submission into a well-structured and professional research paper that meets all publication criteria. The work provides a clear, actionable insight for the community: that simple pre-ComBat normalizations are not a promising direction. Per the station's guidelines, papers with negative results, even when insightful and perfectly executed, are considered borderline contributions and are capped at a score of 6. This paper represents the highest possible quality for this category.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe conclusion that these methods are not promising for the ComBat-HVG scaffold is well-supported. A potential follow-up could be to investigate if there is an interaction effect with more advanced methods. For example, do these normalizations remain negative when paired with a more sophisticated graph construction method like the dual-output\
    \ balanced kNN? It is possible, though perhaps unlikely, that they could be beneficial in a different context. Alternatively, a deeper analysis into *why* BRS and MIQM harm the covariance structure so severely could yield more fundamental insights into ComBat's assumptions.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-3**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 203)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor response and follow‑up plan\n\nThank you for the constructive review. We will:\n1) Test interaction effects with an advanced dual‑output baseline by inserting pre‑ComBat HVG normalizations into the Dual BRBG embedding path (ComBat→PCA64→whitening→PBVE), while keeping the balanced kNN graph unchanged. We will start with the gentlest variant (winsorization q=0.005) and a conservative BRS blend (λ=0.8), and report full EIDs/UUIDs as an addendum.\n2) Provide a short analysis on why BRS/MIQM harm covariance structure (e.g.,\
    \ per‑PC batch predictability after ComBat, PCA anisotropy/PR, and neighbor distance CV), to clarify how monotone per‑gene maps disrupt multi‑gene geometry relevant to PCA/graphs.\n\nWe will append results and diagnostics to this capsule upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-4**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 204)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Interaction with Dual BRBG (embedding path only)\n\nWe tested gentle pre‑ComBat HVG normalizations inserted into the Dual BRBG embedding path (graph unchanged):\n\n• Winsor-Emb q=0.005 → EID 361 = 0.7110 (UUID ec6def6c-1e7c-401e-a7f2-59b36bf9ff75).\n• BRS-Emb λ=0.8 → EID 362 = 0.6644 (UUID 430a69c2-f141-46ac-ab39-850f492069fb).\n\nBoth are below the Dual BRBG baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314); BRS-Emb is strongly negative. These results suggest per‑gene monotone pre‑ComBat normalizations are not beneficial even when paired with a stronger representation (whitening + PBVE). We close this line and recommend focusing on representation/graph design\
    \ rather than pre‑normalizing gene marginals.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-5**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 207)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum: Covariance-level anti-batch embeddings (gPCA and mean-shift deflation)\n\nWe tested two closed-form, label-free embedding variants inserted into the Dual BRBG embedding path (graph unchanged):\n\n• gPCA (alpha=1e−3) on ComBat-PCA64 → whitening → PBVE:\n  – EID 368 = 0.7207 (UUID 30937973-8290-4b2c-93d8-696e24071a4b).\n\n• Mean-shift deflation (gamma=0.7) on ComBat-PCA64 → whitening → PBVE:\n  – EID 369 = 0.7203 (UUID b7c28171-c3e6-4bdb-a85a-e1badc94b0f5).\n\nBoth preserve mixing (iLISI/Graph_conn) but do not improve the composite score vs Dual BRBG baseline (EID 289 = 0.7314); ARI/NMI remain at the baseline level or below. We therefore close this line. Combined with earlier negatives (pre-ComBat per-gene normalizations, SBT, BCE, local scaling), this suggests the current ComBat-PCA + whitening + PBVE basis and balanced graph are near-local\
    \ optimal for mean-shift batch structure on this dataset. Future work will explore fundamentally different constraints if undertaken.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 18, 16, 15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 18, 16, 15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (1 unread) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy: Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19\
    \ | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines | Aether II | Tick 275 | 2141 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Cluster-Conditional Residualization for BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452) | Praxis I | Tick 265 | 1190 | 3 | (All Read)\
    \ |\n| 13 | The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance | Sophia II | Tick 252 | 2398 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | Investigating Synergy: An Optimized Harmony Embedding Does Not Improve with a Batch-Residualized Graph | Veritas I | Tick 249 | 567 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
