role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 367\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Daedalus III\n- Agent Description: Continuing the Daedalus legacy with a focus on methodical analysis and innovation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 156,784 / 300,000 used (52%)\n- Agent Age: 107 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #22: Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis II, Created at Tick: 365\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We introduce a simple, dataset‑agnostic extension to quota‑balanced kNN graphs for single‑cell RNA‑seq batch integration. In a standard Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) + Batch‑Residualized representation (BRBG) pipeline, we modulate each cell’s fraction of cross‑batch neighbors using a local density proxy measured in the residualized PC space. Dense regions mix more across batches; sparse/boundary regions preserve more within‑batch edges.\
    \ Edges remain binary and true distances are recorded. On the Human Heart dataset (~20k cells, 2k HVGs), the method improves the composite score from ~0.724 to 0.7335, and to 0.7365 when paired with a commonly used residualization slope (λ=0.7). We report full metric breakdowns, sensitivity analyses, and negative controls demonstrating robustness and simplicity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-1**\nAuthor: Praxis II (Tick 365)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nBatch effects confound single‑cell RNA‑seq (scRNA‑seq) analyses by introducing unwanted technical variation across studies. Integration methods must remove batch signal while preserving true biological structure. A strong and widely used pattern is Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC): compute a low‑dimensional embedding, correct batch effects in that space, and then analyze with a neighborhood\
    \ graph. In this station’s baseline, an Embed‑then‑Correct embedding is paired with a Batch‑Residualized representation for graph construction (BRBG) and a balanced, quota‑based kNN graph (BBSG). Previous analyses indicate a “robust ridge”: many local hyperparameter tweaks (e.g., PC counts, small neighbor changes, simple edge reweighting) are neutral or negative. We therefore seek a principled but minimal modification that adapts graph connectivity to local structure without adding complexity.\n\n2. Methods\n2.1. Dataset and Preprocessing\n- Dataset: Human Heart (~20,000 cells; 4 batches; ~2,000 highly variable genes).\n- Input: raw UMI counts. Preprocessing: library‑size normalization (target sum 1e4) + log1p; highly variable genes selected with a batch‑aware Seurat v3 procedure (n_top_genes=2,000).\n\n2.2. Embed‑then‑Correct (EtC) Embedding\n- Feature‑weighted PCA (variance‑only): genes are scaled by w_g = var_g^{-α/2} with α=0.5 on HVGs, then PCA(60) is computed.\n- ComBat is applied\
    \ to the PC scores to reduce batch‑aligned shifts in the embedding. The resulting embedding (X_emb) is used for analysis/visualization and to compute neighbors for downstream QC; it is not used to construct the BRBG graph.\n\n2.3. Batch‑Residualized Representation (BRBG) for Graphs\n- Graph representation Z: PCA(50) is computed on HVGs (post normalization/log1p).\n- Per‑PC batch prediction via ridge: Ẑ = B W, where B is one‑hot batches and W = argmin ||Z − B W||₂² + l2||W||₂² (l2=1e‑3).\n- Per‑PC shrinkage: define R²_j = var(Ẑ_j)/var(Z_j). Shrink the batch‑predicted component with γ_j = min(γ_max, λ·R²_j) and form Z_corr = Z − γ ⊙ Ẑ.\n  • Unless stated otherwise, γ_max=0.74. We also evaluate λ=0.9 (baseline) and λ=0.7 (common in recent high‑scoring pipelines).\n\n2.4. Balanced kNN Graph (BBSG)\n- Build a kNN graph of total degree k_total=48 per cell in Z_corr using cosine distance.\n- Enforce per‑batch quotas (equal split across the other batches) for each cell’s neighbors and perform\
    \ a single‑pass column‑balancing step to limit hubness. The graph is stored with binary connectivities (1.0 on edges) and true edge distances; neighborhoods are symmetrized.\n\n2.5. Proposed: Density‑Adaptive Cross‑Batch Quotas\n- Rationale: Equal cross‑batch quotas are globally fair but locally blind. In dense regions, stronger cross‑batch mixing is typically safe; in sparse/boundary regions it can erode label structure.\n- Local density proxy: for each cell i, compute d_k(i), the distance to its k_density‑th nearest neighbor in Z_corr across all batches (we use k_density=30). Normalize to [0,1] and define inv_dense(i) = 1 − d_norm(i) (higher = denser).\n- Cross‑batch fraction per cell: base_cross = (B−1)/B (e.g., 0.75 for 4 batches); set\n    f_cross(i) = clip(base_cross + (inv_dense(i) − 0.5)·2·δ, 0.60, 0.90),\n  where δ controls modulation strength (δ ∈ [0,1)). We then allocate q_cross = round(f_cross·k_total) cross‑batch neighbors across other batches (favoring closer batches) and\
    \ q_within = k_total − q_cross within‑batch neighbors, preserving column balancing and binary edges. No other changes to BBSG are made.\n\n3. Results\nWe report the composite score (mean of 10 normalized metrics) and the normalized per‑metric values in the fixed order specified by the evaluator: ASW_batch, ASW_label, ARI, NMI, Graph_conn, kBET, iLISI, cLISI, PCR, Cell_cycle. All runs used the same dataset and evaluator.\n\n3.1. Baseline and Proposed (λ=0.9)\n- Baseline EtC–BRBG–BBSG (equal quotas): 0.7237 (EID 567). Metrics:\n    0.659, 0.248, 0.725, 0.676, 0.956, 0.788, 0.896, 0.951, 1.000, 0.336.\n- Density‑adaptive quotas (δ=0.15): 0.7335 (EID 593). Metrics:\n    0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.710, 0.946, 0.789, 0.930, 0.966, 1.000, 0.327.\n  Gains are driven by ARI/NMI while preserving batch mixing and connectivity.\n\n3.2. Synergy with λ=0.7\n- Density‑adaptive quotas (δ=0.15) with λ=0.7: 0.7365 (EID 600). Metrics:\n    0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.711, 0.946, 0.791, 0.930, 0.966, 1.000, 0.352.\n\
    \  This setting achieved the best composite score among our tests.\n\n3.3. Sensitivity Analyses\n- Around δ=0.15 with λ=0.7:\n    • δ=0.12 → 0.7320 (EID 603): 0.659, 0.248, 0.761, 0.711, 0.946, 0.788, 0.947, 0.966, 1.000, 0.294.\n    • δ=0.16 → 0.7317 (EID 604): 0.659, 0.248, 0.761, 0.711, 0.946, 0.789, 0.926, 0.966, 1.000, 0.310.\n  These confirm a peak near δ≈0.15.\n- Around δ=0.15 with λ=0.9:\n    • δ=0.10 → 0.7221 (EID 596).\n    • δ=0.18 → 0.7280 (EID 601): 0.659, 0.248, 0.760, 0.710, 0.946, 0.789, 0.918, 0.966, 1.000, 0.284.\n  Too‑weak or too‑strong modulation reduces the composite.\n- γ_max ablation under λ=0.7, δ=0.15:\n    • γ_max=0.75 → 0.7338 (EID 605). Slightly worse than γ_max=0.74; we retain γ_max=0.74.\n\n3.4. Negative Controls (Graph‑Side Alternatives)\n- Two‑scale BRBG (split PCs by batch R² with differential corrections) underperformed:\n    • Full on top 10, zero on others: 0.7128 (EID 594).\n    • Full on top 10, gentle on others: 0.7178 (EID 595).\n  This suggests\
    \ that coarse PC‑level separation is less effective than localized quota adaptivity.\n\n4. Discussion\nThe proposed density‑adaptive rule introduces a small, bounded degree of local adaptivity to a proven graph construction. By “mixing where safe” (dense regions) and preserving within‑batch edges near likely boundaries (sparse regions), it improves label‑structure metrics (ARI/NMI) without harming batch mixing (kBET/iLISI) or connectivity. The rule respects the evaluator’s constraints (binary edges, true distances; no tuned edge weights) and preserves the simplicity and dataset‑agnostic nature of BBSG. Sensitivity analyses indicate a clear optimum near δ≈0.15; γ_max remains best at 0.74. The method synergizes with λ=0.7, a residualization slope commonly used in higher‑scoring pipelines.\n\n5. Reproducibility\nAll experiments were executed via the station’s Research Counter on the same dataset and evaluator. Key evaluation IDs (EIDs) for full logs and metric breakdowns:\n- Baseline equal‑quota\
    \ graph: EID 567 (0.7237).\n- Density‑adaptive δ=0.15 (λ=0.9): EID 593 (0.7335).\n- Density‑adaptive δ=0.15 (λ=0.7, best): EID 600 (0.7365).\n- Sensitivity: EIDs 603 (δ=0.12, 0.7320), 604 (δ=0.16, 0.7317), 596 (δ=0.10, 0.7221), 601 (δ=0.18, 0.7280).\n- γ_max ablation (0.75): EID 605 (0.7338).\nThese EIDs can be reviewed in the Research Counter to validate configuration, metrics, and execution logs.\n\n6. Limitations and Future Work\nThis study evaluates one organ; cross‑organ validation is recommended. Alternative local proxies (e.g., reachability‑based density) and principled global quotas (e.g., OT‑guided batch flows) merit investigation under the same constraints. We caution against heavy PC‑level corrections or edge reweighting, which were neutral/negative in our tests.\n\n7. Conclusion\nA minimal density‑adaptive extension to quota‑balanced graphs—modulating each cell’s cross‑batch fraction by local density in the residualized PC space—consistently improves a strong EtC–BRBG baseline\
    \ on Human Heart. With δ≈0.15 and λ=0.7, we achieve a composite score of 0.7365, improving label structure while preserving batch mixing and connectivity, and staying within a simple, reproducible design.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 366)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an exemplary methods paper that is clear, rigorous, and impactful. The agent introduces a novel, well-motivated, and principled modification to a strong baseline, leading to a significant performance improvement. The submission meets all basic criteria flawlessly, with comprehensive and verifiable experiments, excellent clarity in the methods description, and a professional structure. The work is a model for how to develop and present a new technique, providing a valuable contribution to the station's research by demonstrating how local structural information can be leveraged to improve\
    \ global graph-based batch integration.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent contribution that successfully improves the graph construction phase. The paper itself notes that cross-organ validation is a key next step. Beyond that, a promising research direction would be to explore more principled ways of defining 'local density' and 'mixing safety.' Your current k-NN distance proxy is effective and simple, but you could investigate whether concepts from manifold learning or optimal transport could provide a more robust, parameter-free way to guide local mixing, potentially leading to even greater gains in preserving delicate biological structures at batch boundaries.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nPraxis II replied to public capsule \"Breaking the Ridge: Principled, non-local modifications to EtC–BRBG–BBSG\" (#2) (message #public_2-9):\n\nTo reply, use `/execute_action{goto\
    \ public_memory}` then `/execute_action{reply 2}`.\nTo mute, use `/execute_action{goto public_memory}` then `/execute_action{mute 2}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 22` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 22. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 22 | Density‑Adaptive Quota Balancing Improves Batch‑Residualized Graphs for scRNA‑seq Integration | Praxis II | Tick 365 | 1279 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 21 | Robustness of an Embed-then-Correct Pipeline for scRNA-seq Batch Integration on Human Heart | Praxis II | Tick 343 | 1640 | 4 | (1 unread) |\n| 20 | Negative Synergy:\
    \ Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) Embedding Degrades Performance with Adaptive Residualization Graph | Sophia II | Tick 337 | 1735 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | Systematic Optimization of Adaptive Residualization in the EtC-BRBG Pipeline | Daedalus III | Tick 334 | 686 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | [Final Corrected Version] A Systematic Dissection and Optimization of a Dual-Output Batch Integration Method | Logos III | Tick 328 | 655 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 17 | Feature-Weighted PCA Improves Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Pipeline | Sophia II | Tick 295 | 1830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | BBSG: Column-Balanced Quota Graph Improves EtC-BRBG to 0.7362 on Human Heart | Praxis I | Tick 286 | 835 | 4 | (All Read) |\n| 15 | Batch-Specific Quantile Normalization and Winsorization as Preprocessing Steps for scRNA-seq Batch Integration: Differential Impact on ComBat and Embed-then-Correct Pipelines | Aether II | Tick 275 | 2141 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Cluster-Conditional Residualization for\
    \ BRBG Is Ineffective on Human Heart: A Reproducible Negative Result (EIDs 442, 445, 448, 449, 451, 452) | Praxis I | Tick 265 | 1190 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | The Impact of Latent Correlation Minimization on Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration Performance | Sophia II | Tick 252 | 2398 | 3 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 367
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have read Praxis II''s excellent paper on their "Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing" method. The paper is thorough and provides a crucial piece of information that was not in the public discussion: the results of the "Two-scale graph" experiments.


    **The Decisive Result:**


    Praxis II''s paper contains a "Negative Controls" section (Section 3.4) which reports the results of the two-scale graph experiments:

    *   **EID 594 (Full/Zero Correction):** Score `0.7128`

    *   **EID 595 (Full/Gentle Correction):** Score `0.7178`


    Both are catastrophic failures. The score drop is massive, far below even their baseline of `~0.724`. The "Two-scale graph" idea, which seemed so promising and was the focus of our collaborative effort, is a definitive dead end.


    **A New Research Landscape (Again):**


    This changes everything. The one promising avenue for a major architectural breakthrough has been decisively closed. The only method that has shown any positive result recently is Praxis II''s "Density-adaptive" method, which is a clever but incremental improvement to the graph construction, yielding a score of `0.7365`. This is still significantly below the robust SOTA of `~0.74`.


    The station is back on the performance plateau, and the path forward is once again unclear. I must process this new information. I will read the latest message from Praxis II in the public capsule, and then retreat to my Private Memory to update my log and formulate a new strategy.


    /execute_action{goto public_memory}

    /execute_action{read 2}'
