tick: 368
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have read the final message in the public capsule. Praxis II is continuing to refine their successful \"Density-adaptive\" method, exploring different parameters for the density proxy. This is solid, incremental science.\n\nHowever, the devastating news from their paper remains: the \"Two-scale graph,\" our great hope for a breakthrough, is a failure.\n\nMy collaborative effort has concluded in a dead end. My independent \"Alternative Embeddings\" program also failed. The station is truly at an impasse. The robust SOTA is my `~0.74` score, and no one has found a way to reliably surpass it. Praxis II's new method, while good, is still a step-down at `0.7365`.\n\nIt is time for a radical re-evaluation. The Codex is clear: \"If the whole Station faces prolonged stagnation...it is generally better to step back from the SOTA method and innovate on more promising but lower-scoring baselines.\"\n\nThe entire station has been hyper-focused on the EtC-BRBG architecture. We have optimized\
    \ it, dissected it, and pushed it to its limits. It is time to look elsewhere.\n\nI will go back to the Archive and look at the very earliest papers, at ideas that were perhaps discarded before the EtC paradigm became dominant. There may be a seed of an idea there that was abandoned prematurely.\n\nFirst, I must document this major pivot in my research log.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"The Plateau is Real: A Pivot to Foundational Research\"\ncontent: |\n  **Tick 368: The End of an Era**\n\n  **\"Two-Scale Graph\" Failure:** Praxis II's paper (Archive #22) has confirmed that the \"Two-scale graph\" idea was a catastrophic failure, with scores dropping to ~0.71. Our primary hope for a breakthrough is gone.\n\n  **The Plateau is Confirmed:** The station is at a true performance plateau. My `~0.74` SOTA remains the high water mark. All recent, sophisticated ideas (FWPCA, SCA, Two-scale graphs) have failed to surpass it. Praxis II's\
    \ new \"Density-adaptive\" method is the only recent success, but it is an incremental improvement that scores lower than my SOTA.\n\n  **A Radical Pivot:** The EtC-BRBG framework has been exhausted. In accordance with the Codex, I am now officially stepping back from SOTA optimization. I will begin a new phase of foundational research, seeking inspiration for a completely new architecture.\n\n  **New Program: Archaeological Review:** I will return to the Archive Room and conduct an \"archaeological\" review of the earliest papers in the station's history. My goal is to find a promising, lower-scoring baseline or a discarded idea that was abandoned before the EtC paradigm's dominance. It is time to find a new hill to climb.\n```"
